<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line ltx_leqno">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Monodromy of Projective Curves<span class="ltx_pubnotes"><span class="ltx_pubnotes_content"><span class="ltx_pubnote ltx_role_thanks"><span class="ltx_note_name">Thanks: </span>The first author was partially supported by:
1) MIUR PRIN 2003:
<span id="id1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Spazi di moduli e teoria di Lie</span>;
2) Gnsaga; 3) Far 2002 (PV):
<span id="id2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Varietà algebriche, calcolo algebrico, grafi orientati e
topologici.</span>
The second author was partially supported by
MIUR PRIN 2002 <span id="id3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Geometria e classificazione delle varietà proiettive complesse</span>.
</span></span></span></h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Gian Pietro Pirola and Enrico Schlesinger
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_address"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Address: </span>Dipartimento di Matematica ”F. Casorati”, Universitá di Pavia,
via Ferrata 1, 27100 Pavia, Italia
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email address: </span><a href="mailto:pirola@dimat.unipv.it">pirola@dimat.unipv.it</a>
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_address"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Address: </span>Dipartimento di Matematica, Politecnico di Milano, Piazza Leonardo da
Vinci 32, 20133 Milano, Italia
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email address: </span><a href="mailto:enrsch@mate.polimi.it">enrsch@mate.polimi.it</a>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">The uniform position principle states that, given an irreducible nondegenerate
curve <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\subset\mathbb{P}^{r}(\mathbb{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\subset\mathbb{P}^{r}(\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math>,
a general <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r\!-\!2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.052em">−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r\!-\!2)</annotation></semantics></math>-plane <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\subset\mathbb{P}^{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>r</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\subset\mathbb{P}^{r}</annotation></semantics></math> is <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">uniform</span>, that is,
projection from <math id="abstract1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> induces a rational map <math id="abstract1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\dashrightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">⇢</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\dashrightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>
whose monodromy group is the full symmetric group.
In this paper we first show the locus of non-uniform <math id="abstract1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r-2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r-2)</annotation></semantics></math>-planes has
codimension at least two in the Grassmannian. This result is sharp because,
if there is a point <math id="abstract1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathbb{P}^{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>r</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathbb{P}^{r}</annotation></semantics></math> such that projection from <math id="abstract1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> induces
a map <math id="abstract1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\dashrightarrow\mathbb{P}^{r-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">⇢</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\dashrightarrow\mathbb{P}^{r-1}</annotation></semantics></math> that is not birational onto its image,
then the Schubert cycle <math id="abstract1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(x)</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="abstract1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r\!-\!2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.052em">−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r\!-\!2)</annotation></semantics></math>-planes through <math id="abstract1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>
is contained in the locus of non-uniform subspaces. For a smooth curve <math id="abstract1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>
in <math id="abstract1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math>, we show any irreducible surface of non-uniform lines is a Schubert cycle
<math id="abstract1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(x)</annotation></semantics></math> as above, unless <math id="abstract1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is a rational curve of degree three, four or six.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Key words and phrases: </h6>monodromy groups, Galois groups, projective curves
</div>
<div class="ltx_classification"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_classification">2000 Mathematics Subject Classification</h6>14H30,14H50
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The monodromy group of a branched covering of smooth complex curves
has been object of research since the early days of
algebraic geometry. Zariski <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite> showed that, for a general smooth
complex projective
curve <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> of genus <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g&gt;6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g&gt;6</annotation></semantics></math>, the monodromy group of any covering
<math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is not solvable.
This result has been greatly generalized recently and it is now known that,
for a general curve <math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> of genus <math id="S1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g&gt;3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g&gt;3</annotation></semantics></math>, the monodromy group of a degree
<math id="S1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> indecomposable covering <math id="S1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is either <math id="S1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>,
in which case we say the covering is <span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">uniform</span>,
or <math id="S1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>. As far as existence,
it is well known that a general (resp. every) curve <math id="S1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> of genus <math id="S1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> admits a degree <math id="S1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> uniform
covering <math id="S1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq\frac{g+2}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq\frac{g+2}{2}</annotation></semantics></math> (resp. if <math id="S1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq g+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq g+2</annotation></semantics></math>), while only
recently it has been established that
a general (resp. every) curve <math id="S1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> of genus <math id="S1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> admits a degree <math id="S1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>
covering <math id="S1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> with monodromy group <math id="S1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>
if <math id="S1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 2g+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 2g+1</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> (resp. if <math id="S1.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 12g+4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>12</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 12g+4</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Recently some interesting results have appeared
on the monodromy groups of coverings obtained projecting a smooth plane curve from a point.
Miura and Yoshihara <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite> have determined all groups
occurring as monodromy groups of projections of smooth plane curves of degree <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\leq 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\leq 5</annotation></semantics></math>.
Cukierman <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> has shown that, for a general smooth plane curve
<math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\subset\mathbb{P}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\subset\mathbb{P}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> of degree <math id="S1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> and for every point <math id="S1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathbb{P}^{2}\smallsetminus C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>∖</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathbb{P}^{2}\smallsetminus C</annotation></semantics></math>,
projection from <math id="S1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> induces a <span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">uniform</span> covering <math id="S1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper we begin a systematic study of coverings obtained by projections of
curves embedded in projective space.
Our starting point is the classical <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">uniform position principle</span>.
Consider a degree <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> irreducible algebraic curve
<math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\subset\mathbb{P}^{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>r</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\subset\mathbb{P}^{r}</annotation></semantics></math> that is not contained in a hyperplane.
The uniform position principle, in the formulation
of Joe Harris <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>, states
that, if <math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> is the set of hyperplanes meeting <math id="S1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> transversally,
then the monodromy group of the covering</p>
<table id="S1.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:\{(x,H)\in C\times U:\;\;x\in H\}\rightarrow U\,,\,\,\,\,\;\;\;\pi(x,H)=H," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.838em">:</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="1.687em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:\{(x,H)\in C\times U:\;\;x\in H\}\rightarrow U\,,\,\,\,\,\;\;\;\pi(x,H)=H,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">is the full symmetric group <math id="S1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This statement is equivalent - by a Lefschetz type theorem on
the fundamental group due to Zariski <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite> -
to the assertion that a <span id="S1.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">general</span> <math id="S1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r\!-\!2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.052em">−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r\!-\!2)</annotation></semantics></math>-plane <math id="S1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\subset\mathbb{P}^{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>r</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\subset\mathbb{P}^{r}</annotation></semantics></math>
is uniform, that is, projection from <math id="S1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> exhibits
the normalization <math id="S1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{C}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> as a uniform covering of <math id="S1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>
(cf. Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmthm4" title="Proposition 2.4. ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Monodromy of Projective Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Our first result is</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.1</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In the Grassmannian <math id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{G}(r\!-\!2,\mathbb{P}^{r})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.052em">−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{G}(r\!-\!2,\mathbb{P}^{r})</annotation></semantics></math>
the locus of non-uniform subspaces
has codimension at least two.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Note that this theorem strengthen the uniform position principle.
For smooth curves it is an easy result (cf. Remark <a href="#S3.Thmthm1" title="Remark 3.1. ‣ 3. The locus of non-uniform subspaces has codimension at least two ‣ Monodromy of Projective Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>), but for
curves with arbitrary singularities the proof is more involved
and is carried out in Section <a href="#S3" title="3. The locus of non-uniform subspaces has codimension at least two ‣ Monodromy of Projective Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, where we also consider subspaces
that meet <math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, for a curve in <math id="S1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, our result says
only finitely many points of <math id="S1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, including those lying on <math id="S1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>,
may fail to be uniform.
The theorem is sharp because there are curves with
codimension two families of non-uniform subspaces.
To see this,
observe a subspace <math id="S1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is certainly not uniform if it is
<span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">decomposable</span>, that is, the projection <math id="S1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{L}:\widetilde{C}\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{L}:\widetilde{C}\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>
factors as the composition of two morphisms
of degree at least two. And this is the case if <math id="S1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> contains
a non birational point <math id="S1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, that is, a point <math id="S1.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> such that projection from
<math id="S1.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> induces a morphism <math id="S1.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{x}:\widetilde{C}\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{r-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{x}:\widetilde{C}\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{r-1}</annotation></semantics></math> that is not birational
onto its image. Thus the Schubert cycle <math id="S1.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(x)</annotation></semantics></math>
of <math id="S1.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r\!-\!2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.052em">−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r\!-\!2)</annotation></semantics></math>-planes through a non birational point <math id="S1.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> is contained in
the non-uniform locus.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In Section <a href="#S4" title="4. Families of decomposable subspaces ‣ Monodromy of Projective Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> we give strong restrictions on families of decomposable
<math id="S1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r\!-\!2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.052em">−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r\!-\!2)</annotation></semantics></math>-planes. For a non rational curve <math id="S1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\subset\mathbb{P}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\subset\mathbb{P}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math>, we show
any codimension two irreducible family of decomposable lines
is in fact a Schubert cycle <math id="S1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(x)</annotation></semantics></math> for some non birational point <math id="S1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathbb{P}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathbb{P}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math>.
More generally, we prove:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.2</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose <math id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\subset\mathbb{P}^{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>r</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\subset\mathbb{P}^{r}</annotation></semantics></math> is a nondegenerate irreducible curve,
and <math id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma\!\subset\!\mathbb{G}(r\!-\!2,\mathbb{P}^{r})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">⊂</mo><mrow><mi>𝔾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.052em">−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma\!\subset\!\mathbb{G}(r\!-\!2,\mathbb{P}^{r})</annotation></semantics></math> is an
irreducible closed subscheme of dimension <math id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\!-\!1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.052em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\!-\!1</annotation></semantics></math> such that</span></p>
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">the general subspace </span><math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\in\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\in\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> does not meet </span><math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and is decomposable;</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">every hyperplane </span><math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> in </span><math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{r}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> contains at least one subspace </span><math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\in\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\in\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then <math id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is rational.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">In the case of <span id="S1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">smooth</span> curves <math id="S1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math>, we can actually
classify pairs <math id="S1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(C,\Sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(C,\Sigma)</annotation></semantics></math>
where <math id="S1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is a surface of non-uniform lines for <math id="S1.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>. The result
we prove in Sections <a href="#S5" title="5. Surfaces of non-uniform lines for curves in 
        
          
            
            P
            3 ‣ Monodromy of Projective Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> and <a href="#S6" title="6. Rational space curves admitting surfaces of decomposable lines ‣ Monodromy of Projective Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> is</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.3</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose
<math id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\subset\mathbb{P}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\subset\mathbb{P}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> is a smooth irreducible nondegenerate curve,
<math id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma\subset\mathbb{G}(1,\mathbb{P}^{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>𝔾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma\subset\mathbb{G}(1,\mathbb{P}^{3})</annotation></semantics></math> is an irreducible surface,
and the general line in <math id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> does not meet <math id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> and is
not uniform.
Then one of the following three possibilities holds:</span></p>
<ol id="S1.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">either there exists a non birational point
</span><math id="S1.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathbb{P}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathbb{P}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> such that </span><math id="S1.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is the Schubert cycle </span><math id="S1.I2.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(x)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> of
lines through </span><math id="S1.I2.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">; or</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a twisted cubic curve, and the general line in
</span><math id="S1.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is the intersection of two osculating planes to </span><math id="S1.I2.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">; or</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a rational curve of degree </span><math id="S1.I2.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> (resp. </span><math id="S1.I2.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">), the general line </span><math id="S1.I2.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> in
</span><math id="S1.I2.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is the intersection of two bitangent (resp. tritangent) planes to </span><math id="S1.I2.i3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,
and the projection </span><math id="S1.I2.i3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{L}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is decomposable in the form
</span><math id="S1.I2.i3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{C}\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\beta}}{{\rightarrow}}\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>β</mi></mover><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{C}\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\beta}}{{\rightarrow}}\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">
where </span><math id="S1.I2.i3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> has degree </span><math id="S1.I2.i3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">In fact, every rational curve of degree <math id="S1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> admits a surface of non-uniform
lines as in the theorem. The rational sextic curve studied in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>
admits a surface of non-uniform lines, but we show this is not
the case for a general rational sextic - see Theorem <a href="#S6.Thmthm10" title="Theorem 6.10. ‣ 6. Rational space curves admitting surfaces of decomposable lines ‣ Monodromy of Projective Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.10</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">We close the paper, in Section <a href="#S7" title="7. Examples of curves admitting a one dimensional family of non uniform lines ‣ Monodromy of Projective Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, giving examples of one dimensional families
of non-uniform lines for curves <math id="S1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> of arbitrary genus.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">We work over the field <math id="S1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math> of complex numbers. If <math id="S1.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S1.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>-vector
space, we denote by <math id="S1.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(V)</annotation></semantics></math> the projective space of lines in <math id="S1.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>.
The sentence <span id="S1.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">for a general point <math id="S1.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> in an algebraic variety <math id="S1.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math></span>
means for all points <math id="S1.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> in some Zariski open dense subset of <math id="S1.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p11.1" class="ltx_p">We would like to thank Irene Sabadini and Hisao Yoshihara for helpful conversation,
and Sonia Brivio who carefully read a preliminary version of the manuscript.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Preliminaries</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We collect in this preliminary section some well known results
about branched coverings of algebraic curves. For the convenience of the reader,
we include proofs of some of these results.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We begin recalling
the definition of the <span id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Galois group</span> or <span id="S2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">monodromy group</span>
of a finite morphism of algebraic varieties <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>.
Suppose <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> are irreducible algebraic varieties of the
same dimension over <math id="S2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:X\rightarrow Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:X\rightarrow Y</annotation></semantics></math> is a
generically finite dominant morphism of degree <math id="S2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the
function field <math id="S2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K(X)</annotation></semantics></math> is a finite algebraic extension of <math id="S2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K(Y)</annotation></semantics></math> of
degree <math id="S2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{K}</annotation></semantics></math> denote a Galois closure of <math id="S2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K(X)/K(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K(X)/K(Y)</annotation></semantics></math>. We define
the Galois group <math id="S2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=G_{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>π</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=G_{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math> of the morphism <math id="S2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:X\rightarrow Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:X\rightarrow Y</annotation></semantics></math> to be the Galois
group of the extension <math id="S2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{K}/K(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{K}/K(Y)</annotation></semantics></math>:
the group <math id="S2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> consists of the automorphisms of the
field <math id="S2.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{K}</annotation></semantics></math> that leave every element of <math id="S2.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K(Y)</annotation></semantics></math> fixed. Given a general
point <math id="S2.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\in Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\in Y</annotation></semantics></math>, the group <math id="S2.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> acts faithfully on the fibre
<math id="S2.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{-1}(q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{-1}(q)</annotation></semantics></math> and thus may be regarded as a subgroup of
<math id="S2.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mbox{Aut}(\pi^{-1}(q))\cong S_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>Aut</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mbox{Aut}(\pi^{-1}(q))\cong S_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, and as such it is a transitive subgroup.
There is an equivalent, more geometric description of the Galois group. We can
choose a Zariski open dense subset <math id="S2.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\!\subset\!Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">⊂</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\!\subset\!Y</annotation></semantics></math> such that the
restriction of <math id="S2.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.p2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{-1}(U)\rightarrow U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{-1}(U)\rightarrow U</annotation></semantics></math> is étale, thus a
<math id="S2.p2.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-sheeted covering in the classical topology. If <math id="S2.p2.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> is a point
of <math id="S2.p2.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>, lifting loops at <math id="S2.p2.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.p2.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{-1}(U)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{-1}(U)</annotation></semantics></math> we obtain the
monodromy representation <math id="S2.p2.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho:\pi_{1}(U,q)\rightarrow\mbox{Aut}(\pi^{-1}(q))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mtext>Aut</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho:\pi_{1}(U,q)\rightarrow\mbox{Aut}(\pi^{-1}(q))</annotation></semantics></math>. It is not difficult to see that the image of
<math id="S2.p2.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>, that is, the monodromy group of the covering, is isomorphic to
the Galois group <math id="S2.p2.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, and is therefore independent of the
choice of <math id="S2.p2.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, Proposition p. 689]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:X\rightarrow Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:X\rightarrow Y</annotation></semantics></math> be a generically finite dominant morphism of degree <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>
between complex algebraic varieties. We say that <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> is <span id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">uniform</span>
if the monodromy group of <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> is the full symmetric group <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We say that <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> is <span id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">decomposable</span> if there exists an open dense subset <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\subseteq Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\subseteq Y</annotation></semantics></math>
over which <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> factors as</p>
<table id="S2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{-1}(U)\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\alpha}}{{\rightarrow}}V\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\beta}}{{\rightarrow}}U" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>α</mi></mover><mi>V</mi><mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>β</mi></mover><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{-1}(U)\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\alpha}}{{\rightarrow}}V\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\beta}}{{\rightarrow}}U</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> are finite morphisms
of degree at least <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 2.2</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">One knows <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> is decomposable if and only if there is an intermediate field
in the extension <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K(X)/K(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K(X)/K(Y)</annotation></semantics></math>, and this is equivalent to the condition the
Galois group <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math> be imprimitive. Furthermore:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S2.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> is uniform, then it is indecomposable.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> is indecomposable and <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math> contains a
transposition, then <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> is uniform.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The first statement is clear. Suppose <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{\pi}\!\subset\!S_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">⊂</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{\pi}\!\subset\!S_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>
contains a transposition, and let <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\subseteq G_{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>π</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\subseteq G_{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math>
be the subgroup generated by the transpositions contained in <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> is a nontrivial normal subgroup of <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> is not transitive,
then <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math> is imprimitive and <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> is decomposable.
On the other hand, if <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> is transitive, then <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> is uniform because
a transitive subgroup of <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> generated by transpositions must be all of
<math id="S2.Thmthm2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> - see for example <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Throughout the paper <math id="S2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> denotes an irreducible reduced curve of degree <math id="S2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>
in <math id="S2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{r}</annotation></semantics></math> - except in the Section <a href="#S6" title="6. Rational space curves admitting surfaces of decomposable lines ‣ Monodromy of Projective Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> where we treat rational curves of even degree <math id="S2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2d</annotation></semantics></math>.
We assume <math id="S2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is nondegenerate, that is, not contained in a hyperplane. We denote by <math id="S2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{C}</annotation></semantics></math>
the normalization of <math id="S2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, by <math id="S2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\widetilde{C}\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>r</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\widetilde{C}\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{r}</annotation></semantics></math> the normalization map followed by the embedding of <math id="S2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>
in <math id="S2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{r}</annotation></semantics></math>, and by <math id="S2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|V|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|V|</annotation></semantics></math> the base point free <math id="S2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{r}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{r}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>
on <math id="S2.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{C}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponding to <math id="S2.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>. We have <math id="S2.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|V|=\mathbb{P}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|V|=\mathbb{P}(V)</annotation></semantics></math> where
<math id="S2.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\subseteq\mbox{H}^{0}(\widetilde{C},f^{*}{\mathcal{O}}_{\mathbb{P}^{r}}(1))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>r</mi></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\subseteq\mbox{H}^{0}(\widetilde{C},f^{*}{\mathcal{O}}_{\mathbb{P}^{r}}(1))</annotation></semantics></math> is the subspace
generated by the restrictions of the linear forms on <math id="S2.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{r}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{C}</annotation></semantics></math>.
There is a one to one correspondence between hyperplanes <math id="S2.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>
in <math id="S2.p3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{r}=\mathbb{P}(V^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{r}=\mathbb{P}(V^{*})</annotation></semantics></math> and divisors of the linear series <math id="S2.p3.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|V|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|V|</annotation></semantics></math>, and
we let <math id="S2.p3.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{H}=f^{*}(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{H}=f^{*}(H)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the divisor corresponding to <math id="S2.p3.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>.
If <math id="S2.p3.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is transversal to <math id="S2.p3.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S2.p3.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{H}</annotation></semantics></math> may be identified with
the intersection of <math id="S2.p3.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p3.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Given a linear subspace <math id="S2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\subset\mathbb{P}^{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>r</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\subset\mathbb{P}^{r}</annotation></semantics></math> of codimension <math id="S2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, projection from <math id="S2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is the morphism <math id="S2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{M}:\mathbb{P}^{r}\!\smallsetminus\!M\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{s-1}_{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.052em">∖</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{M}:\mathbb{P}^{r}\!\smallsetminus\!M\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{s-1}_{M}</annotation></semantics></math>
mapping a point <math id="S2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\notin M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∉</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\notin M</annotation></semantics></math> to
the linear subspace generated by <math id="S2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{s-1}_{M}\cong\mathbb{P}^{s-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>≅</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{s-1}_{M}\cong\mathbb{P}^{s-1}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the Schubert cycle of codimension <math id="S2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\!-\!1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0.052em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\!-\!1</annotation></semantics></math>
subspaces of <math id="S2.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{r}</annotation></semantics></math> containing <math id="S2.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>. Projection from <math id="S2.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> induces
a morphism <math id="S2.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{M}:\widetilde{C}\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{s-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{M}:\widetilde{C}\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{s-1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">For a codimension two subspace <math id="S2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>, the morphism <math id="S2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{L}:\widetilde{C}\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{L}:\widetilde{C}\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> is finite (because <math id="S2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is nondegenerate) of degree <math id="S2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{L}=d-\deg(D_{L})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{L}=d-\deg(D_{L})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> is the base locus of the linear
system <math id="S2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{D_{H}:H\supset L\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⊃</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{D_{H}:H\supset L\}</annotation></semantics></math>. We let <math id="S2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the monodromy
group of <math id="S2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{L}</annotation></semantics></math>. We say <math id="S2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is <span id="S2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">uniform</span> (resp. <span id="S2.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">decomposable</span>)
if the morphism <math id="S2.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> is uniform (resp. decomposable).</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 2.3</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{G}(r\!-\!2,\mathbb{P}^{r})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.052em">−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{G}(r\!-\!2,\mathbb{P}^{r})</annotation></semantics></math> the Grassmannian of
codimension two subspaces of <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{r}</annotation></semantics></math>. The set of uniform subspaces
(with respect to a given curve <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>) is constructible in
<math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{G}(r\!-\!2,\mathbb{P}^{r})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.052em">−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{G}(r\!-\!2,\mathbb{P}^{r})</annotation></semantics></math>, and it contains a non-empty open
set (cf. <a href="#S2.Thmthm4" title="Proposition 2.4. ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Monodromy of Projective Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a> below).</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The projections <math id="S2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{L}</annotation></semantics></math>, as <math id="S2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> varies in the set of <math id="S2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r\!-\!2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.052em">−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r\!-\!2)</annotation></semantics></math> -planes
that do not meet <math id="S2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, are restrictions of the global projection:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi:I=\{(P,H)\in\widetilde{C}\times\mathbb{P}^{r*}:\;\;f(P)\in H\}\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{r*},\,\,\,\,(P,H)\mapsto H." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.838em">:</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.847em">,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:I=\{(P,H)\in\widetilde{C}\times\mathbb{P}^{r*}:\;\;f(P)\in H\}\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{r*},\,\,\,\,(P,H)\mapsto H.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p6.2" class="ltx_p">Note that <math id="S2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{r-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{r-1}</annotation></semantics></math>-bundle over <math id="S2.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{C}</annotation></semantics></math>. In fact, from the Euler sequence
one sees <math id="S2.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> is the projective bundle of <span id="S2.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">lines</span> in the fibres of
<math id="S2.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}\Omega_{\mathbb{P}^{r}}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>r</mi></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}\Omega_{\mathbb{P}^{r}}(1)</annotation></semantics></math>. By construction the scheme theoretic fibre of <math id="S2.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>
over <math id="S2.p6.m11" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H\in\mathbb{P}^{r*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\in\mathbb{P}^{r*}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}(H)=D_{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>H</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}(H)=D_{H}</annotation></semantics></math>; thus <math id="S2.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> is a finite flat surjective morphism
(= a branched covering) of degree <math id="S2.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=\deg(C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=\deg(C)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">We use the well known fact <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, p.111]</cite>
that the monodromy of <math id="S2.p7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi:I\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{r*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:I\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{r*}</annotation></semantics></math> is the full symmetric
group <math id="S2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> to show the general codimension two subspace
<math id="S2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is uniform - for smooth curves this is proven for example in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>.
More precisely we have:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2.4</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\!\subset\!\mathbb{P}^{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>r</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\!\subset\!\mathbb{P}^{r}</annotation></semantics></math> be a codimension two subspace that does not meet <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>,
and let <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{red}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{red}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the
branch divisor of <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi:I\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{r*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:I\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{r*}</annotation></semantics></math> taken with its reduced scheme structure.
If the line <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> meets <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{red}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{red}</annotation></semantics></math> transversally, then <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is uniform.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">Given a codimension two subspace <math id="S2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>, the codomain of the projection
<math id="S2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> is the line <math id="S2.p8.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}_{L}\!\subset\!\mathbb{P}^{r*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>L</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.108em">⊂</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}_{L}\!\subset\!\mathbb{P}^{r*}</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S2.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> does not meet <math id="S2.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, then
<math id="S2.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{-1}(\mathbb{P}^{1}_{L})\cong\widetilde{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{-1}(\mathbb{P}^{1}_{L})\cong\widetilde{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, and under this isomorphism the restriction
of <math id="S2.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{-1}(\mathbb{P}^{1}_{L})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{-1}(\mathbb{P}^{1}_{L})</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to <math id="S2.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{L}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">The monodromy group of <math id="S2.p9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi:I\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{r*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:I\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{r*}</annotation></semantics></math> is the full symmetric
group <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, Lemma p.111]</cite>. Since <math id="S2.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> meets <math id="S2.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{red}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{red}</annotation></semantics></math>
transversally, by a Lefschetz-type theorem due to
Zariski <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite> (see also <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, p. 34]</cite>
and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>), the inclusion <math id="S2.p9.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}_{L}\hookrightarrow\mathbb{P}^{r*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>L</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}_{L}\hookrightarrow\mathbb{P}^{r*}</annotation></semantics></math> induces an epimorphism <math id="S2.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{1}(U\cap\mathbb{P}^{1}_{L})\rightarrow\pi_{1}(U)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>∩</mo><msubsup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{1}(U\cap\mathbb{P}^{1}_{L})\rightarrow\pi_{1}(U)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.p9.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="U=\mathbb{P}^{r*}\!\smallsetminus\!B_{red}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.052em">∖</mo><mi>B</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U=\mathbb{P}^{r*}\!\smallsetminus\!B_{red}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence
the monodromy of <math id="S2.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> is the full symmetric group as well.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 2.5</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We will see below the branch divisor <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> contains the dual variety <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> whose points
are the hyperplanes tangent to <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>. We recall the definition of <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> and the biduality theorem.
Let <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="W^{0}_{C}\subset\mathbb{P}^{r}\times\mathbb{P}^{r*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>C</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>r</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{0}_{C}\subset\mathbb{P}^{r}\times\mathbb{P}^{r*}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the set of pairs <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,H)</annotation></semantics></math> where
<math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> is a smooth point of <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is a hyperplane containing the tangent line
<math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{x}C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{x}C</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{C}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the closure of <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{0}_{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>C</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{0}_{C}</annotation></semantics></math>. We say that a hyperplane <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>
is tangent to <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> at a (not necessarily smooth) point <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> if the pair <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,H)</annotation></semantics></math> belongs to
<math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{C}</annotation></semantics></math>. The dual variety <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m18" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="C^{*}\!\subset\!\mathbb{P}^{r*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.108em">⊂</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{*}\!\subset\!\mathbb{P}^{r*}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as the set of hyperplanes
that are tangent to <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., as the projection of <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{C}</annotation></semantics></math>
in <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. Of course, these definitions apply to any subvariety of <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{r}</annotation></semantics></math>. The biduality
theorem asserts <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{C}=W_{C^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{C}=W_{C^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math>, that is, a hyperplane <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is tangent to <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> if and
only if in the dual projective space the hyperplane <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is tangent to <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> at
the point <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 2.6</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In fact, the hypothesis <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> does not meet
<math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is redundant in Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmthm4" title="Proposition 2.4. ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Monodromy of Projective Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a>: one can check, for example using the biduality
theorem, that the fact <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> meets <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{red}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{red}</annotation></semantics></math> transversally implies <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> does not meet
<math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p10.1" class="ltx_p">We now wish to describe the branch divisor <math id="S2.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>, and the subspaces
<math id="S2.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> meeting <math id="S2.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{red}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{red}</annotation></semantics></math> non transversally. For this we need to introduce
the <span id="S2.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">ramification sequence</span> of the linear series <math id="S2.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|V|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|V|</annotation></semantics></math> at
a point <math id="S2.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\in\widetilde{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∈</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\in\widetilde{C}</annotation></semantics></math> as defined in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>.
Since <math id="S2.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r+1</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional, for a given <math id="S2.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\in\widetilde{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∈</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\in\widetilde{C}</annotation></semantics></math> the ”multiplicity at <math id="S2.p10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> function”
<math id="S2.p10.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\mapsto m_{P}(D_{H})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\mapsto m_{P}(D_{H})</annotation></semantics></math>
takes on exactly <math id="S2.p10.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r+1</annotation></semantics></math>-values, so there are nonnegative integers
<math id="S2.p10.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{0}(P)\leq\alpha_{1}(P)\leq\cdots\leq\alpha_{r}(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">≤</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{0}(P)\leq\alpha_{1}(P)\leq\cdots\leq\alpha_{r}(P)</annotation></semantics></math> such that</p>
<table id="S2.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{m_{P}(D_{H}):\ H^{*}\in\mathbb{P}(V)\}=\{\alpha_{0},1+\alpha_{1},2+\alpha_{2}\ldots,r+\alpha_{r}\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.778em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{m_{P}(D_{H}):\ H^{*}\in\mathbb{P}(V)\}=\{\alpha_{0},1+\alpha_{1},2+\alpha_{2}\ldots,r+\alpha_{r}\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p10.2" class="ltx_p">For each <math id="S2.p10.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=0,1,\ldots,r-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=0,1,\ldots,r-1</annotation></semantics></math> the set of hyperplanes <math id="S2.p10.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> with</p>
<table id="S2.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{P}(D_{H})\geq k+1+\alpha_{k+1}(P)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{P}(D_{H})\geq k+1+\alpha_{k+1}(P)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p10.3" class="ltx_p">is a linear subspace <math id="S2.p10.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{*}_{k,P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{*}_{k,P}</annotation></semantics></math>
of <math id="S2.p10.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(V)</annotation></semantics></math> of codimension <math id="S2.p10.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k+1</annotation></semantics></math>. Its dual <math id="S2.p10.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{k}(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{k}(P)</annotation></semantics></math>
is a <math id="S2.p10.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional subspace of <math id="S2.p10.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(V^{*})=\mathbb{P}^{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>r</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(V^{*})=\mathbb{P}^{r}</annotation></semantics></math> called the osculating <math id="S2.p10.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-plane
of <math id="S2.p10.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S2.p10.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> - of course, <math id="S2.p10.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}(P)=T_{P}\,C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}(P)=T_{P}\,C</annotation></semantics></math> is the tangent line to the branch of <math id="S2.p10.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>
through <math id="S2.p10.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>. The osculating <math id="S2.p10.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-plane is the unique <math id="S2.p10.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-plane <math id="S2.p10.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> such that</p>
<table id="S2.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{P}(D_{M})=k+1+\alpha_{k+1}(P)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{P}(D_{M})=k+1+\alpha_{k+1}(P)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p10.4" class="ltx_p">(the other <math id="S2.p10.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-planes have smaller multiplicity at <math id="S2.p10.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p11.1" class="ltx_p">One easily verifies <math id="S2.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is tangent to <math id="S2.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S2.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if there
is <math id="S2.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\in\widetilde{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∈</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\in\widetilde{C}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=f(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=f(P)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{P}\,C\!\subset H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.108em">⊂</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{P}\,C\!\subset H</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus <math id="S2.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\in C^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\in C^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>
if and only if there is
<math id="S2.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\in\widetilde{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∈</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\in\widetilde{C}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.p11.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{P}(D_{H})\geq 2+\alpha_{2}(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{P}(D_{H})\geq 2+\alpha_{2}(P)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p12.1" class="ltx_p">We can now describe the branch locus <math id="S2.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.p12.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi:I\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{r*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:I\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{r*}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For each point <math id="S2.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in C</annotation></semantics></math> we let</p>
<table id="S2.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\beta(x)=\sum_{\{P\in\widetilde{C}:f(P)=x\}}\alpha_{1}(P)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∈</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></munder><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta(x)=\sum_{\{P\in\widetilde{C}:f(P)=x\}}\alpha_{1}(P)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p12.2" class="ltx_p">denote the total ramification of <math id="S2.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S2.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2.7</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="C^{*}\!\subset\!\mathbb{P}^{r*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.108em">⊂</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{*}\!\subset\!\mathbb{P}^{r*}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the hypersurface dual to <math id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, and for each
point <math id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathbb{P}^{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>r</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathbb{P}^{r}</annotation></semantics></math> let <math id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Pi_{x}\!\subset\!\mathbb{P}^{r*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>x</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.108em">⊂</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{x}\!\subset\!\mathbb{P}^{r*}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the hyperplane dual to <math id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> denote the branch divisor of <math id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi:I\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{r*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:I\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{r*}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then in the group of Weil
divisors of <math id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{r*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{r*}</annotation></semantics></math> we have:</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="B=C^{*}+\sum_{\{x\in C\}}\beta(x)\Pi_{x}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></munder><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=C^{*}+\sum_{\{x\in C\}}\beta(x)\Pi_{x}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Furthermore, the multiplicity of <math id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> at a point <math id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H^{*}\in\mathbb{P}^{r*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}\in\mathbb{P}^{r*}</annotation></semantics></math> is</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="m_{H^{*}}(B)=\sum_{\{P\in D_{H}\}}(m_{P}(D_{H})-1)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>D</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>H</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></munder><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{H^{*}}(B)=\sum_{\{P\in D_{H}\}}(m_{P}(D_{H})-1)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text">The ramification divisor <math id="S2.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as the scheme of zeros on
<math id="S2.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> of the Jacobian determinant of <math id="S2.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>, and the branch divisor
<math id="S2.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=\pi_{*}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=\pi_{*}(R)</annotation></semantics></math> as the push forward of <math id="S2.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.p13.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{r*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{r*}</annotation></semantics></math>. Equivalently,
<math id="S2.p13.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by the zeroth Fitting ideal of
the relative sheaf of differential <math id="S2.p13.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Omega_{I/\mathbb{P}^{r*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{I/\mathbb{P}^{r*}}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>.
This definition is functorial, as is the push forward of divisors;
it follows that, if <math id="S2.p13.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}_{L}\!\subset\!\mathbb{P}^{r*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>L</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.108em">⊂</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}_{L}\!\subset\!\mathbb{P}^{r*}</annotation></semantics></math>
is a line corresponding to a codimension two subspace <math id="S2.p13.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> not meeting <math id="S2.p13.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>,
the branch divisor <math id="S2.p13.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.p13.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{L}:\pi^{-1}(\mathbb{P}^{1}_{L})\cong\widetilde{C}\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{L}:\pi^{-1}(\mathbb{P}^{1}_{L})\cong\widetilde{C}\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}_{L}</annotation></semantics></math>
is the scheme theoretic intersection of <math id="S2.p13.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p13.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}_{L}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since <math id="S2.p13.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{H}=\sum_{P\in\widetilde{C}}m_{P}(D_{H})P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∈</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{H}=\sum_{P\in\widetilde{C}}m_{P}(D_{H})P</annotation></semantics></math> is the fibre of <math id="S2.p13.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S2.p13.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>
and we work over <math id="S2.p13.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, the branch divisor of <math id="S2.p13.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> is</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="B_{L}=\sum_{\{H^{*}\in\mathbb{P}^{1}_{L}\}}\left(\sum_{\{P\in D_{H}\}}(m_{P}(D_{H})-1)\right)H^{*}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>L</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></munder><mrow><mo>(</mo><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>D</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>H</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></munder><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{L}=\sum_{\{H^{*}\in\mathbb{P}^{1}_{L}\}}\left(\sum_{\{P\in D_{H}\}}(m_{P}(D_{H})-1)\right)H^{*}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p13.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p13.2.1" class="ltx_text">Now the multiplicity of <math id="S2.p13.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S2.p13.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is precisely the multiplicity
of intersection at <math id="S2.p13.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.p13.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> with a general line through <math id="S2.p13.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>,
hence we conclude</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="m_{H^{*}}(B)=\sum_{\{P\in D_{H}\}}(m_{P}(D_{H})-1)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>D</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>H</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></munder><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{H^{*}}(B)=\sum_{\{P\in D_{H}\}}(m_{P}(D_{H})-1).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p13.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p13.3.1" class="ltx_text">If <math id="S2.p13.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is a tangent hyperplane to <math id="S2.p13.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>,
then there is a point <math id="S2.p13.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\in\widetilde{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∈</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\in\widetilde{C}</annotation></semantics></math>
appearing with multiplicity at least two in <math id="S2.p13.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{H}</annotation></semantics></math>, thus <math id="S2.p13.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> belongs to <math id="S2.p13.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S2.p13.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is a component of <math id="S2.p13.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>.
Furthermore, since we are in characteristic zero, the general tangent
plane <math id="S2.p13.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.p13.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is simply tangent, that is, <math id="S2.p13.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{H^{*}}(B^{*})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{H^{*}}(B^{*})=1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus <math id="S2.p13.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> is generically smooth along <math id="S2.p13.m39" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, which means that <math id="S2.p13.m40" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is a
reduced component of <math id="S2.p13.m41" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text">On the other hand, suppose <math id="S2.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is in the branch locus but not in <math id="S2.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then in the fibre <math id="S2.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{H}</annotation></semantics></math> there is a point <math id="S2.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="S2.p14.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{P}(D_{H})\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{P}(D_{H})\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> because <math id="S2.p14.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is in the branch locus and
<math id="S2.p14.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{P}(D_{H})=1+\alpha_{1}(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{P}(D_{H})=1+\alpha_{1}(P)</annotation></semantics></math> because <math id="S2.p14.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is not tangent to <math id="S2.p14.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>.
Therefore <math id="S2.p14.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1+\alpha_{1}(P)\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1+\alpha_{1}(P)\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> so that <math id="S2.p14.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{P}(D_{K})\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{P}(D_{K})\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> for every
hyperplane <math id="S2.p14.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> through <math id="S2.p14.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=f(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=f(P)</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence the entire hyperplane <math id="S2.p14.m14" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Pi_{x}\!\subset\!\mathbb{P}^{r*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>x</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.108em">⊂</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{x}\!\subset\!\mathbb{P}^{r*}</annotation></semantics></math>
is contained in the branch locus. To compute the multiplicity of <math id="S2.p14.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{x}</annotation></semantics></math>
in <math id="S2.p14.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>, we pick a hyperplane <math id="S2.p14.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> through <math id="S2.p14.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> that is not tangent to <math id="S2.p14.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> and does
not pass through any other singularity of <math id="S2.p14.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e. <math id="S2.p14.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{x}</annotation></semantics></math>
is the only component of <math id="S2.p14.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> through <math id="S2.p14.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the desired multiplicity
is equal to:</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="m_{H^{*}}(B)=\sum_{\{P\in D_{H}\}}(m_{P}(D_{H})-1)=\sum_{\{P\in\widetilde{C}:f(P)=x\}}\alpha_{1}(P)=\beta(x)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>D</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>H</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></munder><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∈</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></munder><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{H^{*}}(B)=\sum_{\{P\in D_{H}\}}(m_{P}(D_{H})-1)=\sum_{\{P\in\widetilde{C}:f(P)=x\}}\alpha_{1}(P)=\beta(x).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p15.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p15.1.1" class="ltx_text">Note: since <math id="S2.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in C</annotation></semantics></math>, by biduality <math id="S2.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> is tangent
to <math id="S2.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p16.1" class="ltx_p">We can now list the codimension two subspaces <math id="S2.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> for which
<math id="S2.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> does not meet
<math id="S2.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{red}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{red}</annotation></semantics></math> transversally:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.8</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fix a codimension two subspace <math id="S2.Thmthm8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\!\subset\!\mathbb{P}^{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>r</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\!\subset\!\mathbb{P}^{r}</annotation></semantics></math> not meeting <math id="S2.Thmthm8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S2.Thmthm8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{1},\ldots,\Pi_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{1},\ldots,\Pi_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>
be the hyperplanes of <math id="S2.Thmthm8.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{r*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{r*}</annotation></semantics></math> contained in <math id="S2.Thmthm8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S2.Thmthm8.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> meets <math id="S2.Thmthm8.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{red}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{red}</annotation></semantics></math>
transversally unless</span></p>
<ol id="S2.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a contained in a hyperplane </span><math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> with </span><math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}\in C^{*}_{sing}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}\in C^{*}_{sing}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, or</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a contained in a hyperplane </span><math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> with </span><math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}\in C^{*}\cap\Pi_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}\in C^{*}\cap\Pi_{i}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, or</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a contained in a hyperplane </span><math id="S2.I2.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> with </span><math id="S2.I2.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}\in\Pi_{i}\cap\Pi_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}\in\Pi_{i}\cap\Pi_{j}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p17.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p17.1.1" class="ltx_text">The subspace <math id="S2.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies one of the
conditions (1),(2),(3) if and only if <math id="S2.p17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> meets the singular locus of <math id="S2.p17.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{red}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{red}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus we only have to check that if <math id="S2.p17.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> is tangent to <math id="S2.p17.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{red}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{red}</annotation></semantics></math> at a smooth point
<math id="S2.p17.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S2.p17.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> meets <math id="S2.p17.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>. This follows from biduality if <math id="S2.p17.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}\in C^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}\in C^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, while
if <math id="S2.p17.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}\in\Pi_{i}=\Pi_{x_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}\in\Pi_{i}=\Pi_{x_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S2.p17.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, then
<math id="S2.p17.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> is tangent to and hence contained in <math id="S2.p17.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{x_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{x_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>, so that
<math id="S2.p17.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}\in L\cap C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}\in L\cap C</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p18.1" class="ltx_p">We close this preliminary section recalling the relation between
the monodromy group <math id="S2.p18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> and the ramification type of <math id="S2.p18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{L}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2.9</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau:\widetilde{C}\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau:\widetilde{C}\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> be a finite morphism of degree <math id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, and let
<math id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1},\ldots,H_{n}~\in~\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.608em">∈</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1},\ldots,H_{n}~\in~\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> be the branch points of <math id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then to each <math id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> one can associate a permutation <math id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> in the Galois group
<math id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{\tau}\subseteq S_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{\tau}\subseteq S_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> so that</span></p>
<ol id="S2.I3" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S2.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I3.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">the Galois group </span><math id="S2.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I3.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">
is generated by any set of </span><math id="S2.I3.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\!-\!1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.052em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\!-\!1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I3.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> permutations among the </span><math id="S2.I3.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I3.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">;</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S2.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I3.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">if </span><math id="S2.I3.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{j=1}^{t_{i}}m_{ij}P_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{j=1}^{t_{i}}m_{ij}P_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I3.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> denotes
the scheme theoretic fibre of </span><math id="S2.I3.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I3.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> over </span><math id="S2.I3.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{i}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I3.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,
then </span><math id="S2.I3.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I3.i2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a permutation
with cycle structure </span><math id="S2.I3.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(m_{i1},\ldots,m_{it_{i}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(m_{i1},\ldots,m_{it_{i}})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I3.i2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.
Thus, if </span><math id="S2.I3.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{i}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I3.i2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a simple branch point, the permutation </span><math id="S2.I3.i2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I3.i2.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a
transposition.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In particular, if all but one of the branch points are simple, the morphism <math id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> is uniform.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.p19" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p19.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p19.1.1" class="ltx_text">This is classical and explained for example in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, §III.4]</cite>.
It follows from the fact the fundamental group of
<math id="S2.p19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}\smallsetminus\{H_{1},\ldots,H_{n}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}\smallsetminus\{H_{1},\ldots,H_{n}\}</annotation></semantics></math> is generated by small
loops <math id="S2.p19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{1},\ldots,\gamma_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{1},\ldots,\gamma_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> around <math id="S2.p19.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1},\ldots,H_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1},\ldots,H_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>
respectively, with the relation <math id="S2.p19.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\gamma_{1}\gamma_{2}\cdots\gamma_{n}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>γ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>γ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{1}\gamma_{2}\cdots\gamma_{n}=1</annotation></semantics></math>.
If all the branch points but one are simple, the Galois group is generated by
transpositions, hence must be all of <math id="S2.p19.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 2.10</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\subset\mathbb{P}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\subset\mathbb{P}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> be a twisted cubic curve, and for every <math id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\in C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\in C</annotation></semantics></math> let
<math id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(P)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the osculating plane to <math id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>, so that <math id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{H(P)}=3P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{H(P)}=3P</annotation></semantics></math>.
Given two distinct points <math id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P,Q\in C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P,Q\in C</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=H(P)\cap H(Q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=H(P)\cap H(Q)</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(P)</annotation></semantics></math> meets
<math id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> only at <math id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>, the line <math id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> does not meet <math id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, and the projection <math id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{L}</annotation></semantics></math>
has degree <math id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>. By Riemann-Hurwitz, the projection <math id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{L}</annotation></semantics></math>
is ramified only over <math id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(P)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(Q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(Q)</annotation></semantics></math>, and by Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmthm9" title="Proposition 2.9. ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Monodromy of Projective Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.9</span></a> the monodromy
group of <math id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{3}</annotation></semantics></math>. Letting <math id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> vary we find a two dimensional
irreducible family of lines <math id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> that are non-uniform and indecomposable. We will later
show (Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 5.2. ‣ 5. Surfaces of non-uniform lines for curves in 
        
          
            
            P
            3 ‣ Monodromy of Projective Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>) there is no other smooth curve in <math id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> admitting a two
dimensional family of non-uniform indecomposable lines.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm10.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm10.p2.1" class="ltx_p">If we let <math id="S2.Thmthm10.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmthm10.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> come together, the line <math id="S2.Thmthm10.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> becomes tangent to <math id="S2.Thmthm10.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>,
thus <math id="S2.Thmthm10.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> is an isomorphism and <math id="S2.Thmthm10.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is uniform according to our definition.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>The locus of non-uniform subspaces has codimension at least two</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section we prove the locus of non-uniform subspaces has
codimension at least two in the Grassmannian <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{G}(r\!-\!2,\mathbb{P}^{r})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.052em">−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{G}(r\!-\!2,\mathbb{P}^{r})</annotation></semantics></math>.
One easily reduces this statement to the case <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=2</annotation></semantics></math>, and so the problem is to show
a plane curve has only finitely many non-uniform points.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is <span id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">smooth</span> and <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}(C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(C)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the locus of codimension two subspaces
that do not meet <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> and are not uniform, the fact that <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}(C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(C)</annotation></semantics></math> has
codimension at least two is an immediate consequence of
Propositions <a href="#S2.Thmthm7" title="Proposition 2.7. ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Monodromy of Projective Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.7</span></a> and <a href="#S2.Thmthm9" title="Proposition 2.9. ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Monodromy of Projective Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.9</span></a>.
More generally suppose <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is a curve for which the branch divisor <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> of the morphism
<math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi:I\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{r*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:I\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{r*}</annotation></semantics></math> is reduced, and consider a subspace <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}(C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(C)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then by Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmthm9" title="Proposition 2.9. ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Monodromy of Projective Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.9</span></a> there are at least two branch points <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>
of <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> that are not simple.
By Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmthm7" title="Proposition 2.7. ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Monodromy of Projective Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.7</span></a> the hyperplanes <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are singular points
of the branch divisor <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence
<math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> meets the singular locus <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{sing}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{sing}</annotation></semantics></math> of the branch divisor in at
least two distinct points. Therefore, if <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the locus of lines
<math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathbb{P}^{1}_{L}:L\in\mathcal{N}(C)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathbb{P}^{1}_{L}:L\in\mathcal{N}(C)\}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</p>
<table id="S3.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim\mathcal{N}(C)=\dim\mathcal{N}^{*}\leq 2\dim B_{sing}\leq 2r-4," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim\mathcal{N}(C)=\dim\mathcal{N}^{*}\leq 2\dim B_{sing}\leq 2r-4,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">thus <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}(C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(C)</annotation></semantics></math> has codimension at least two in the Grassmannian.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We now generalize the above statement to curves <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> with arbitrary singularities,
without excluding subspaces that meet <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>. The key case is that of plane curves.
When <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\!=\!2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.108em">=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\!=\!2</annotation></semantics></math>, a codimension two subspace is a point <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathbb{P}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathbb{P}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.p2.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}_{x}\!\subset\!\mathbb{P}^{2*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>x</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.108em">⊂</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}_{x}\!\subset\!\mathbb{P}^{2*}</annotation></semantics></math> is the pencil of lines
through <math id="S3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>. We first consider the case <math id="S3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\notin C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∉</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\notin C</annotation></semantics></math> (outer points
in the terminology of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>). Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmthm4" title="Proposition 2.4. ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Monodromy of Projective Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a>
implies that <math id="S3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> is uniform, unless <math id="S3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> belongs to one of the the
finitely many lines listed in <a href="#S2.Thmthm8" title="Lemma 2.8. ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Monodromy of Projective Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.8</span></a>. Thus, if we can show
that the general point of an arbitrary line is uniform, we can
conclude that there are only finitely many non-uniform outer
points. We will need the following lemma:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau:X\rightarrow Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau:X\rightarrow Y</annotation></semantics></math> be a surjective étale morphism
of smooth algebraic varieties, and let <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h:Y\rightarrow U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h:Y\rightarrow U</annotation></semantics></math> be a dominant morphism.
Assume that, for the general point <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\in U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\in U</annotation></semantics></math>,
the fibres <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{q}=(h\circ\tau)^{-1}(q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{q}=(h\circ\tau)^{-1}(q)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{q}=h^{-1}(q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{q}=h^{-1}(q)</annotation></semantics></math> are
irreducible. Then, for a general point <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\in U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\in U</annotation></semantics></math>, the monodromy group
of <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{q}:X_{q}\rightarrow Y_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{q}:X_{q}\rightarrow Y_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> is a normal subgroup of the monodromy group
of <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau:X\rightarrow Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau:X\rightarrow Y</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">By generic smoothness
(cf. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, Lemma 1.5a)]</cite>) there is a dense Zariski open subset
<math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{0}\!\subset\!U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">⊂</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{0}\!\subset\!U</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h:h^{-1}(U_{0})\rightarrow U_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h:h^{-1}(U_{0})\rightarrow U_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is a fibre bundle
in the analytic topology. Thus we may assume that <math id="S3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h:Y\rightarrow U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h:Y\rightarrow U</annotation></semantics></math> is a fibre
bundle in the analytic topology. Let <math id="S3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g:Y_{q}\rightarrow Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g:Y_{q}\rightarrow Y</annotation></semantics></math> be the
inclusion of the fibre over <math id="S3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\in U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\in U</annotation></semantics></math>. The long homotopy sequence
of the fibration <math id="S3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h:Y\rightarrow U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h:Y\rightarrow U</annotation></semantics></math> shows that <math id="S3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{*}\pi_{1}(Y_{q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{*}\pi_{1}(Y_{q})</annotation></semantics></math> is a
normal subgroup of <math id="S3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{1}(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{1}(Y)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Now, fixed a base point <math id="S3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in Y_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in Y_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>, the monodromy representations
of <math id="S3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> are related by the commutative diagram:</span></p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{CD}\pi_{1}(Y_{q},y)@&gt;{\rho(\tau_{q},y)}&gt;{}&gt;\mbox{Aut}(\tau_{q}^{-1}(y))\\
@V{}V{g_{*}}V@V{}V{}V\\
\pi_{1}(Y,y)@&gt;{\rho(\tau,y)}&gt;{}&gt;\mbox{Aut}(\tau^{-1}(y))\end{CD}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mover><mover class="ltx_horizontally_stretchy"><mo class="ltx_horizontally_stretchy">→</mo><mspace style="width:30.0pt;" width="30.0pt"></mspace></mover><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mover></mtd><mtd><mrow><mtext>Aut</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mi>q</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo mathsize="1.600em" stretchy="false">↓</mo><mpadded style="width:0.0pt;" width="0.0pt"><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">g</mi><mo mathsize="0.710em">∗</mo></msub></mpadded></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd><mo mathsize="1.600em" stretchy="false">↓</mo></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mover><mover class="ltx_horizontally_stretchy"><mo class="ltx_horizontally_stretchy">→</mo><mspace style="width:30.0pt;" width="30.0pt"></mspace></mover><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mover></mtd><mtd><mrow><mtext>Aut</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{CD}\pi_{1}(Y_{q},y)@&gt;{\rho(\tau_{q},y)}&gt;{}&gt;\mbox{Aut}(\tau_{q}^{-1}(y))\\
@V{}V{g_{*}}V@V{}V{}V\\
\pi_{1}(Y,y)@&gt;{\rho(\tau,y)}&gt;{}&gt;\mbox{Aut}(\tau^{-1}(y))\end{CD}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">where the vertical arrow on the right is an isomorphism.
Since <math id="S3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{*}\pi_{1}(Y_{q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{*}\pi_{1}(Y_{q})</annotation></semantics></math> is a normal subgroup of <math id="S3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{1}(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{1}(Y)</annotation></semantics></math>,
the diagram above shows the monodromy group <math id="S3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mbox{Im}(\rho(\tau_{q},y))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Im</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mbox{Im}(\rho(\tau_{q},y))</annotation></semantics></math>
of <math id="S3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> is a normal subgroup of the monodromy group
<math id="S3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mbox{Im}(\rho(\tau,y))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Im</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mbox{Im}(\rho(\tau,y))</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.3</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> be a line in <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then all but finitely many points
<math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in M</annotation></semantics></math> are uniform.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Fix a general point <math id="S3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>.
We prove that the monodromy group <math id="S3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>
of <math id="S3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> is the full
symmetric group <math id="S3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>
showing it contains a transposition and it is a normal subgroup of <math id="S3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.
To see <math id="S3.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> contains a transposition is immediate: it is enough to observe
a general point <math id="S3.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> lies on a simple tangent line to <math id="S3.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">To see <math id="S3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> is normal, we use the previous lemma. For this, we need to
consider the map <math id="S3.p6.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi:I\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{2*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:I\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{2*}</annotation></semantics></math> of Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmthm4" title="Proposition 2.4. ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Monodromy of Projective Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a>
as a family of projections indexed by the points of <math id="S3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>.
To this end we fix a second line <math id="S3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> be the point
of intersection of <math id="S3.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, and write
<math id="S3.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{\prime}=L\smallsetminus\{z_{0}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{\prime}=L\smallsetminus\{z_{0}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{\prime}=M\smallsetminus\{z_{0}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{\prime}=M\smallsetminus\{z_{0}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the map <math id="S3.p6.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="L^{\prime}\times M^{\prime}\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{2*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>M</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{\prime}\times M^{\prime}\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{2*}</annotation></semantics></math>
sending a pair <math id="S3.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t,u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t,u)</annotation></semantics></math> to the line joining them is an open embedding.
Let <math id="S3.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> be the open subscheme <math id="S3.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{\prime}\times M^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{\prime}\times M^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.p6.m14" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{2*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{2*}</annotation></semantics></math> and define</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="X=\pi^{-1}(L^{\prime}\times M^{\prime}),\,\,\,\tau=\pi_{|X}:X\rightarrow Y." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.677em">,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=\pi^{-1}(L^{\prime}\times M^{\prime}),\,\,\,\tau=\pi_{|X}:X\rightarrow Y.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p6.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S3.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> is open in <math id="S3.p6.m16" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{2*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{2*}</annotation></semantics></math>, the monodromy group of
<math id="S3.p6.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> coincides with the monodromy group of <math id="S3.p6.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., it is the symmetric group <math id="S3.p6.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Now we restrict <math id="S3.p6.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> to an open subset over which <math id="S3.p6.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> is étale,
and apply Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmthm2" title="Lemma 3.2. ‣ 3. The locus of non-uniform subspaces has codimension at least two ‣ Monodromy of Projective Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> to <math id="S3.p6.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> with
<math id="S3.p6.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U=M^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U=M^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> to conclude that, for general
<math id="S3.p6.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\in M^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\in M^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, the monodromy group of <math id="S3.p6.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{q}:X_{q}\rightarrow Y_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{q}:X_{q}\rightarrow Y_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> is normal
in <math id="S3.p6.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Now it is immediate to verify that
<math id="S3.p6.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> is isomorphic to an open subscheme of <math id="S3.p6.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{C}</annotation></semantics></math>,
and with this identification <math id="S3.p6.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{q}:X_{q}\rightarrow Y_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{q}:X_{q}\rightarrow Y_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> is projection from <math id="S3.p6.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus the monodromy group <math id="S3.p6.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> is normal in <math id="S3.p6.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This finishes the proof.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 3.4</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The same argument shows that, for a nondegenerate curve <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\subset\mathbb{P}^{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>r</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\subset\mathbb{P}^{r}</annotation></semantics></math>,
given any hyperplane <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{r}</annotation></semantics></math>, the general codimension two subspace
<math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> contained in <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is uniform.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">We can also show, with an argument similar to the proof of <a href="#S3.Thmthm3" title="Proposition 3.3. ‣ 3. The locus of non-uniform subspaces has codimension at least two ‣ Monodromy of Projective Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>,
that only finitely many points of <math id="S3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> (inner points) may fail to be uniform:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.5</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A general point of <math id="S3.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is uniform.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">Fix a general point <math id="S3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> and
a general line <math id="S3.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>.
The fibre of <math id="S3.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S3.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\cap H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\cap H</annotation></semantics></math> with the point <math id="S3.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> removed, therefore
the monodromy group <math id="S3.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> is a subgroup of <math id="S3.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{d-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{d-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. We may assume
<math id="S3.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=\deg(C)\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=\deg(C)\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>. Then we can find a simple tangent to <math id="S3.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> passing with multiplicity
one through <math id="S3.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>. It follows the monodromy of <math id="S3.p8.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> contains a transposition.
Thus it is enough to show the monodromy group <math id="S3.p8.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> is normal
in <math id="S3.p8.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{d-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{d-1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">To this end, fix a line <math id="S3.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S3.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the set of
smooth points of <math id="S3.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> that do not belong to <math id="S3.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>; similarly let <math id="S3.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the
set of points of <math id="S3.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> that do not lie on <math id="S3.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S3.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{\prime}\times C^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{\prime}\times C^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is an open
subscheme of the incidence correspondence</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="I=\{(P,H)\in\widetilde{C}\times\mathbb{P}^{2*}:\;\;f(P)\in H\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.838em">:</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I=\{(P,H)\in\widetilde{C}\times\mathbb{P}^{2*}:\;\;f(P)\in H\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p9.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p9.2.1" class="ltx_text">via
the embedding <math id="S3.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t,x)\mapsto(f^{-1}(x),\overline{xt})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t,x)\mapsto(f^{-1}(x),\overline{xt})</annotation></semantics></math>.
The projection <math id="S3.p9.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi:L^{\prime}\times C^{\prime}\subset I\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{2*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>C</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>⊂</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:L^{\prime}\times C^{\prime}\subset I\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{2*}</annotation></semantics></math>
maps a pair <math id="S3.p9.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t,x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t,x)</annotation></semantics></math> to the line <math id="S3.p9.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{xt}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{xt}</annotation></semantics></math>, and of course we know
<math id="S3.p9.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> is generically finite of degree <math id="S3.p9.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> with monodromy group <math id="S3.p9.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">In order to be able to apply Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmthm2" title="Lemma 3.2. ‣ 3. The locus of non-uniform subspaces has codimension at least two ‣ Monodromy of Projective Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>
we need to pull back the covering <math id="S3.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{\prime}\times C^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{\prime}\times C^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. This pull back, that is
the fibred product</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="T=(L^{\prime}\times C^{\prime})\times_{\mathbb{P}^{2*}}(L^{\prime}\times C^{\prime})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=(L^{\prime}\times C^{\prime})\times_{\mathbb{P}^{2*}}(L^{\prime}\times C^{\prime}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p10.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p10.2.1" class="ltx_text">has two components: one is the diagonal, the other is isomorphic to</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="Z=\{(x,y)\in C^{\prime}\times C^{\prime}:x\neq y;\,\overline{xy}\cap L\in L^{\prime}\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>y</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">;</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>∩</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z=\{(x,y)\in C^{\prime}\times C^{\prime}:x\neq y;\,\overline{xy}\cap L\in L^{\prime}\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p10.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p10.3.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S3.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{Z}:Z\rightarrow L^{\prime}\times C^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{Z}:Z\rightarrow L^{\prime}\times C^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the composition of the inclusion
<math id="S3.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\hookrightarrow T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\hookrightarrow T</annotation></semantics></math> with the projection <math id="S3.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="pr_{1}:T\rightarrow L^{\prime}\times C^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">pr_{1}:T\rightarrow L^{\prime}\times C^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, that is,
<math id="S3.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{Z}(x,y)=(\overline{xy}\cap L,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>∩</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{Z}(x,y)=(\overline{xy}\cap L,y)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S3.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{Z}:Z\rightarrow L^{\prime}\times C^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{Z}:Z\rightarrow L^{\prime}\times C^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is
generically finite of degree <math id="S3.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d-1</annotation></semantics></math>, and, for general <math id="S3.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\in C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\in C</annotation></semantics></math>, the restriction
<math id="S3.p10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{q}:Z_{q}\rightarrow L^{\prime}\times\,q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.392em">×</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{q}:Z_{q}\rightarrow L^{\prime}\times\,q</annotation></semantics></math>
is (modulo obvious identifications) projection from <math id="S3.p10.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>. Using Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmthm2" title="Lemma 3.2. ‣ 3. The locus of non-uniform subspaces has codimension at least two ‣ Monodromy of Projective Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>
we conclude the monodromy group <math id="S3.p10.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> is a normal subgroup of <math id="S3.p10.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{\pi_{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{\pi_{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and we will be
done if we can show <math id="S3.p10.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{\pi_{Z}}=S_{d-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{\pi_{Z}}=S_{d-1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">Choose an open dense subset <math id="S3.p11.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="Y\subset\mathbb{P}^{2*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\subset\mathbb{P}^{2*}</annotation></semantics></math> such that</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi_{|\pi^{-1}(Y)}:W=\pi^{-1}(Y)\rightarrow Y" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>π</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{|\pi^{-1}(Y)}:W=\pi^{-1}(Y)\rightarrow Y</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p11.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p11.2.1" class="ltx_text">is étale, and fix a base point <math id="S3.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w\in W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w\in W</annotation></semantics></math>. Then as in <a href="#S3.Thmthm2" title="Lemma 3.2. ‣ 3. The locus of non-uniform subspaces has codimension at least two ‣ Monodromy of Projective Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> we look at the
diagram:</span></p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{CD}\pi_{1}(W,w)@&gt;{\rho(\pi_{Z})}&gt;{}&gt;\mbox{Aut}(\pi_{Z}^{-1}(w))\cong S_{d-1}\\
@V{}V{\pi_{*}}V@V{}V{}V\\
\pi_{1}(Y,\pi(w))@&gt;{\rho(\pi)}&gt;{}&gt;\mbox{Aut}(\pi^{-1}(\pi(w)))\cong S_{d}\end{CD}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mover><mover class="ltx_horizontally_stretchy"><mo class="ltx_horizontally_stretchy">→</mo><mspace style="width:30.0pt;" width="30.0pt"></mspace></mover><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mover></mtd><mtd><mrow><mrow><mtext>Aut</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo mathsize="1.600em" stretchy="false">↓</mo><mpadded style="width:0.0pt;" width="0.0pt"><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">π</mi><mo mathsize="0.710em">∗</mo></msub></mpadded></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd><mo mathsize="1.600em" stretchy="false">↓</mo></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mover><mover class="ltx_horizontally_stretchy"><mo class="ltx_horizontally_stretchy">→</mo><mspace style="width:30.0pt;" width="30.0pt"></mspace></mover><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mover></mtd><mtd><mrow><mrow><mtext>Aut</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{CD}\pi_{1}(W,w)@&gt;{\rho(\pi_{Z})}&gt;{}&gt;\mbox{Aut}(\pi_{Z}^{-1}(w))\cong S_{d-1}\\
@V{}V{\pi_{*}}V@V{}V{}V\\
\pi_{1}(Y,\pi(w))@&gt;{\rho(\pi)}&gt;{}&gt;\mbox{Aut}(\pi^{-1}(\pi(w)))\cong S_{d}\end{CD}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p11.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p11.3.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S3.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\rightarrow Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\rightarrow Y</annotation></semantics></math> is a topological covering,
the image of the vertical map <math id="S3.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is the stabilizer of
<math id="S3.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> under the action of <math id="S3.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{1}(Y,\pi(w))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{1}(Y,\pi(w))</annotation></semantics></math> on the fibre <math id="S3.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{-1}(\pi(w))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{-1}(\pi(w))</annotation></semantics></math>.
The bottom row of the diagram is surjective because <math id="S3.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{\pi}=S_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{\pi}=S_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Therefore the image of <math id="S3.p11.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(\pi)\circ\pi_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(\pi)\circ\pi_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is the stabilizer
of <math id="S3.p11.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.p11.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mbox{Aut}(\pi^{-1}(\pi(w)))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Aut</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mbox{Aut}(\pi^{-1}(\pi(w)))</annotation></semantics></math>, which is also the image of
the vertical map on the right. Therefore <math id="S3.p11.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(\pi_{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(\pi_{Z})</annotation></semantics></math> is surjective, that is,
the monodromy group of <math id="S3.p11.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.p11.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{d-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{d-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and this concludes the proof.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p12.1" class="ltx_p">We can now prove:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.6</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\!\subset\!\mathbb{P}^{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>r</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\!\subset\!\mathbb{P}^{r}</annotation></semantics></math> be an
irreducible non degenerate curve, <math id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>. In the Grassmannian
<math id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{G}(r\!-\!2,\mathbb{P}^{r})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.052em">−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{G}(r\!-\!2,\mathbb{P}^{r})</annotation></semantics></math> the locus of non-uniform subspaces has
codimension at least two.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text">Suppose first <math id="S3.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=2</annotation></semantics></math>. We have to show all but finitely many points <math id="S3.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathbb{P}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathbb{P}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are uniform
for <math id="S3.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>.
By Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmthm4" title="Proposition 2.4. ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Monodromy of Projective Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a>, if <math id="S3.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> is non uniform,
then <math id="S3.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> lies either on one of the finitely many lines listed in Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmthm8" title="Lemma 2.8. ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Monodromy of Projective Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.8</span></a> or
on <math id="S3.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>. By Propositions <a href="#S3.Thmthm3" title="Proposition 3.3. ‣ 3. The locus of non-uniform subspaces has codimension at least two ‣ Monodromy of Projective Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> and <a href="#S3.Thmthm5" title="Proposition 3.5. ‣ 3. The locus of non-uniform subspaces has codimension at least two ‣ Monodromy of Projective Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a>
only finitely many such points may fail to be uniform.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text">Now assume <math id="S3.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>. Fix an irreducible nontrivial effective divisor
<math id="S3.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}\subset\mathbb{G}(r\!-\!2,\mathbb{P}^{r})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>𝔾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.052em">−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}\subset\mathbb{G}(r\!-\!2,\mathbb{P}^{r})</annotation></semantics></math>. We show the general
subspace <math id="S3.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> is uniform reducing to the case <math id="S3.p14.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=2</annotation></semantics></math>.
For this, let <math id="S3.p14.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> be a general codimension three subspace, so that
projection from <math id="S3.p14.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{M}:\widetilde{C}\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{2}_{M}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{M}:\widetilde{C}\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{2}_{M}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p14.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p14.2.1" class="ltx_text">is a birational morphism of <math id="S3.p14.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{C}</annotation></semantics></math> onto <math id="S3.p14.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1}=\pi_{M}(\widetilde{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1}=\pi_{M}(\widetilde{C})</annotation></semantics></math>. Then
projecting <math id="S3.p14.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> from a codimension
two subspace <math id="S3.p14.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> containing <math id="S3.p14.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is the same as projecting <math id="S3.p14.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> from the point
<math id="S3.p14.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{M}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{M}(L)</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.p14.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{2}_{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{2}_{M}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We have seen there are only finitely many non-uniform points in <math id="S3.p14.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{2}_{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{2}_{M}</annotation></semantics></math>,
thus we see there are only finitely many codimension two subspaces
<math id="S3.p14.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> that contain <math id="S3.p14.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> and are not uniform.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p15.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p15.1.1" class="ltx_text">To finish, we need to observe </span><span id="S3.p15.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">a general codimension three
subspace </span><math id="S3.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.p15.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> of </span><math id="S3.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.p15.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is contained in infinitely many general subspaces </span><math id="S3.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.p15.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> of
</span><math id="S3.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.p15.1.6" class="ltx_text">. More precisely, let <math id="S3.p15.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(M)\cong\mathbb{P}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(M)\cong\mathbb{P}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> denote
the set of codimension two subspaces <math id="S3.p15.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> that contain a given <math id="S3.p15.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, and fix
a proper subvariety <math id="S3.p15.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma\subset\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma\subset\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>. The
set of codimension three subspaces <math id="S3.p15.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> such that</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim\,\sigma(M)\cap(\mathcal{D}\smallsetminus\Sigma)&gt;0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">dim</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim\,\sigma(M)\cap(\mathcal{D}\smallsetminus\Sigma)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p15.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p15.2.1" class="ltx_text">contains a dense open subset <math id="S3.p15.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\subset\mathbb{G}(r\!-\!3,\mathbb{P}^{r})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>𝔾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.052em">−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\subset\mathbb{G}(r\!-\!3,\mathbb{P}^{r})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Indeed, since every nontrivial effective divisor on the
Grassmannian is ample, the dimension of <math id="S3.p15.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}\cap\sigma(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}\cap\sigma(M)</annotation></semantics></math> is at least
one for every codimension three subspace <math id="S3.p15.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>. On the other hand,
a dimension count on the incidence variety
<math id="S3.p15.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{(M,L):M\subset L\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{(M,L):M\subset L\}</annotation></semantics></math> shows that, for <math id="S3.p15.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> general in <math id="S3.p15.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{G}(r\!-\!3,\mathbb{P}^{r})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.052em">−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{G}(r\!-\!3,\mathbb{P}^{r})</annotation></semantics></math>,
the intersection <math id="S3.p15.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma\cap\sigma(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma\cap\sigma(M)</annotation></semantics></math> is at most zero dimensional.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 3.7</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The bound on the codimension of the family of non-uniform subspaces is sharp.
Indeed, there are curves <math id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> for which there
exist points <math id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathbb{P}^{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>r</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathbb{P}^{r}</annotation></semantics></math> giving a non birational projection. Fix such a point <math id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>.
If <math id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is a codimension two subspace containing <math id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> factors through
<math id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> hence <math id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is decomposable and in particular it is non-uniform. Thus
every codimension two subspace <math id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> through <math id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> is non-uniform, and the family of
such subspaces has codimension two in the Grassmannian.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>Families of decomposable subspaces</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section we show that, if <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\subset\mathbb{P}^{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>r</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\subset\mathbb{P}^{r}</annotation></semantics></math> is not rational,
an irreducible family <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> of decomposable <math id="S4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r\!-\!2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.052em">−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r\!-\!2)</annotation></semantics></math>-planes
either has dimension less than <math id="S4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\!-\!1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.052em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\!-\!1</annotation></semantics></math> or is special in the sense
that a general hyperplane of <math id="S4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{r}</annotation></semantics></math> does not
contain any subspace in <math id="S4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>. More precisely:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.1</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma\!\subset\!\mathbb{G}(r\!-\!2,\mathbb{P}^{r})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">⊂</mo><mrow><mi>𝔾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.052em">−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma\!\subset\!\mathbb{G}(r\!-\!2,\mathbb{P}^{r})</annotation></semantics></math> is an
irreducible closed subscheme of dimension <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\!-\!1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.052em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\!-\!1</annotation></semantics></math> such that</span></p>
<ol id="S4.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">the general subspace </span><math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\in\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\in\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> does not meet </span><math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and is decomposable;</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">every hyperplane </span><math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> in </span><math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{r}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> contains at least one subspace </span><math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\in\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\in\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is rational.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">We give now a sketch of the proof, the rest of the section being
dedicated to filling in the details. A classical theorem of
de Franchis <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> states that there are only finitely many curves
<math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> (up to isomorphisms) for which there exists a surjective morphism <math id="S4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{C}\rightarrow Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{C}\rightarrow Y</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since the projections
<math id="S4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> form a continuous family, it follows we can find a curve <math id="S4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> and an integer
<math id="S4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> such that, for every <math id="S4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> in an open subset <math id="S4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>, the
projection <math id="S4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> factors through some degree <math id="S4.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> morphism <math id="S4.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha:\widetilde{C}\rightarrow Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha:\widetilde{C}\rightarrow Y</annotation></semantics></math>,
where <math id="S4.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> varies in an irreducible family of morphisms <math id="S4.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Now by hypothesis a general hyperplane <math id="S4.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> contains at least one subspace <math id="S4.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\in W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\in W</annotation></semantics></math>,
hence <math id="S4.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\cap H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\cap H</annotation></semantics></math> contains a subset of <math id="S4.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> points that make up a fibre
of some morphism <math id="S4.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>. By the uniform position principle,
any set of <math id="S4.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> points in <math id="S4.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\cap H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\cap H</annotation></semantics></math> is a fibre of some <math id="S4.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">If <math id="S4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(Y)\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(Y)\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, then the set of morphisms <math id="S4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{C}\rightarrow Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{C}\rightarrow Y</annotation></semantics></math> of degree <math id="S4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> is finite
(up to translations in <math id="S4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(Y)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(Y)=1</annotation></semantics></math>), hence we can find a single morphism
<math id="S4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> such that the projections <math id="S4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\in W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\in W</annotation></semantics></math> all factor through <math id="S4.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>.
But then not every set of <math id="S4.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> points in <math id="S4.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\cap H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\cap H</annotation></semantics></math> is a fibre of <math id="S4.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>,
a contradiction. Thus <math id="S4.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\cong\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>≅</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\cong\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">It follows that any two fibres of a morphism <math id="S4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha:\widetilde{C}\rightarrow Y\cong\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>≅</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha:\widetilde{C}\rightarrow Y\cong\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> are linearly
equivalent as divisors on <math id="S4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{C}</annotation></semantics></math>. Now the uniform
position principle implies that in <math id="S4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\cap H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\cap H</annotation></semantics></math> any two disjoint subsets
of cardinality <math id="S4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> are linearly equivalent as divisors on <math id="S4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{C}</annotation></semantics></math>. From this one
deduces <math id="S4.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2P</annotation></semantics></math> is linearly equivalent to <math id="S4.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2Q</annotation></semantics></math> for two general points <math id="S4.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P,Q\in\widetilde{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P,Q\in\widetilde{C}</annotation></semantics></math>,
hence <math id="S4.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is rational.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We now fill in the details of the proof.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 4.2</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is an irreducible variety and
<math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:\widetilde{C}\times T\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}\times T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:\widetilde{C}\times T\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}\times T</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-morphism such that
for every <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in T</annotation></semantics></math> the restriction
<math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{t}:\widetilde{C}\times\{t\}\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}\times\{t\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{t}:\widetilde{C}\times\{t\}\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}\times\{t\}</annotation></semantics></math>
is a decomposable finite morphism of degree <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then there exist an open dense
subset <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\subset T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\subset T</annotation></semantics></math>, a curve <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>, an integer <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\leq e\leq d/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\leq e\leq d/2</annotation></semantics></math>,
and an irreducible family <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> of degree <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> morphisms from <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{C}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>
with the following property:
for every <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in W</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="S4.Thmthm2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> factors through <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">A well known theorem by de
Franchis <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite> states that,
up to isomorphisms,
there are only finitely many smooth projective curves <math id="S4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>,
for which there exists a surjective morphism <math id="S4.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha:\widetilde{C}\rightarrow Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha:\widetilde{C}\rightarrow Y</annotation></semantics></math>. Let
<math id="S4.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mbox{Mor}_{d}(\widetilde{C},\mathbb{P}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>Mor</mtext><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mbox{Mor}_{d}(\widetilde{C},\mathbb{P}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math> denote the quasiprojective scheme
that parametrizes the set of finite morphisms <math id="S4.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{C}\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{C}\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> of degree <math id="S4.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>
(see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, I.1.10]</cite> or <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>); by the universal property of
<math id="S4.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mbox{Mor}_{d}(\widetilde{C},\mathbb{P}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>Mor</mtext><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mbox{Mor}_{d}(\widetilde{C},\mathbb{P}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math>
the assignment <math id="S4.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\mapsto\pi_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\mapsto\pi_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> defines a morphism
<math id="S4.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi:T\rightarrow\mbox{Mor}_{d}(\widetilde{C},\mathbb{P}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>Mor</mtext><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi:T\rightarrow\mbox{Mor}_{d}(\widetilde{C},\mathbb{P}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math>, and by assumption the image <math id="S4.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi(T)</annotation></semantics></math>
is contained in the set of decomposable morphisms.
Now by de Franchis Theorem the set of decomposable morphisms
in <math id="S4.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mbox{Mor}_{d}(\widetilde{C},\mathbb{P}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>Mor</mtext><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mbox{Mor}_{d}(\widetilde{C},\mathbb{P}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math> is covered by finitely many sets, images of maps</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mbox{Mor}_{e_{j}}(\widetilde{C},Y_{i})\times\mbox{Mor}_{d/e_{j}}(Y_{i},\mathbb{P}^{1})\rightarrow\mbox{Mor}_{d}(\widetilde{C},\mathbb{P}^{1}),\;\;\;\;(\alpha,\beta)\mapsto\beta\circ\alpha." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext>Mor</mtext><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mtext>Mor</mtext><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>Mor</mtext><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.287em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mbox{Mor}_{e_{j}}(\widetilde{C},Y_{i})\times\mbox{Mor}_{d/e_{j}}(Y_{i},\mathbb{P}^{1})\rightarrow\mbox{Mor}_{d}(\widetilde{C},\mathbb{P}^{1}),\;\;\;\;(\alpha,\beta)\mapsto\beta\circ\alpha.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p6.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text">where <math id="S4.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> varies in a finite set of curves, and
<math id="S4.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> in the set of integers <math id="S4.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> that divide <math id="S4.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since <math id="S4.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is irreducible,
there is one of these sets that contains an open dense subset
<math id="S4.p6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\subset\psi(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\subset\psi(T)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then we can take <math id="S4.p6.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W=\psi^{-1}(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W=\psi^{-1}(A)</annotation></semantics></math>, and
the rest is clear.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p7.1" class="ltx_p">We will use the following version of the</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_monodromy">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Monodromy Theorem 4.3</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> pp. 111-2)<span id="S4.Thmthm3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\!\subset\!\mathbb{P}^{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>r</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\!\subset\!\mathbb{P}^{r}</annotation></semantics></math> be a reduced irreducible curve of degree <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>,
and denote by <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="U\!\subset\!\mathbb{P}^{r*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">⊂</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\!\subset\!\mathbb{P}^{r*}</annotation></semantics></math> the open set of hyperplanes transverse
to <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>. Then</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="I(d)=\{(P_{1},\ldots,P_{d},H):H\in U;\mbox{the $P_{i}$'s are distinct
and $D_{H}=P_{1}+\cdots+P_{d}$ }\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>;</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">the </mtext><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">’s are distinct
and </mtext><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> </mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(d)=\{(P_{1},\ldots,P_{d},H):H\in U;\mbox{the $P_{i}$'s are distinct
and $D_{H}=P_{1}+\cdots+P_{d}$ }\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">is an irreducible variety.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Note that the symmetric group <math id="S4.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> acts on the covering projection
<math id="S4.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p:I(d)\rightarrow U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p:I(d)\rightarrow U</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> is the quotient of <math id="S4.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(d)</annotation></semantics></math> modulo the action
of <math id="S4.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. It follows that for any subgroup <math id="S4.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, the quotient
space <math id="S4.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(d)/G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(d)/G</annotation></semantics></math> is an irreducible variety. In particular, we have:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 4.4</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every positive integer <math id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{C}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{C}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the <math id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-th symmetric
product of <math id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, which we identify with the set of effective divisors of
degree <math id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{C}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the sets</span></p>
<ol id="S4.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S4.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S4.Ex22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{e}=\{(E,H)\in\widetilde{C}_{e}\times U:\;E\leq D_{H}\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.558em">:</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>H</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{e}=\{(E,H)\in\widetilde{C}_{e}\times U:\;E\leq D_{H}\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S4.Ex23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{e,f}=\{(E,F,H)\in\widetilde{C}_{e}\times\widetilde{C}_{f}\times U:\;E+F\leq D_{H}\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.558em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>H</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{e,f}=\{(E,F,H)\in\widetilde{C}_{e}\times\widetilde{C}_{f}\times U:\;E+F\leq D_{H}\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">are irreducible.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{e}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text"> is the quotient of <math id="S4.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(d)</annotation></semantics></math> by the subgroup of <math id="S4.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> that stabilizes the
subset <math id="S4.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,\ldots,e\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,\ldots,e\}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,\ldots,d\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,\ldots,d\}</annotation></semantics></math>; while <math id="S4.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{e,f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{e,f}</annotation></semantics></math>
is the quotient of <math id="S4.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(d)</annotation></semantics></math> by the subgroup of <math id="S4.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> that stabilizes the
subsets <math id="S4.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,\ldots,e\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,\ldots,e\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{e+1,\ldots,e+f\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{e+1,\ldots,e+f\}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.p9.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,\ldots,d\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,\ldots,d\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmthm1" title="Theorem 4.1. ‣ 4. Families of decomposable subspaces ‣ Monodromy of Projective Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S4.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">Note that by assumption <math id="S4.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2)</annotation></semantics></math> the morphism</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p:J=\{(L,H)\in\Sigma\times\mathbb{P}^{r*}:\;L\!\subset\!H\}\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{r*},\;\;\;\;(L,H)\mapsto H" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>J</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.558em">:</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">⊂</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msup><mo rspace="1.287em">,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p:J=\{(L,H)\in\Sigma\times\mathbb{P}^{r*}:\;L\!\subset\!H\}\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{r*},\;\;\;\;(L,H)\mapsto H</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p10.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p10.2.1" class="ltx_text">is dominant and generically finite. It follows that every open dense subset
<math id="S4.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\subset\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\subset\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> has the property that, given a general hyperplane <math id="S4.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>, we
can find <math id="S4.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> that is contained in <math id="S4.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">Thus under the hypotheses of the theorem
we can find <math id="S4.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\!\subset\!\mathbb{G}(r\!-\!2,\mathbb{P}^{r})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">⊂</mo><mrow><mi>𝔾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.052em">−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\!\subset\!\mathbb{G}(r\!-\!2,\mathbb{P}^{r})</annotation></semantics></math>
irreducible of dimension <math id="S4.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\!-\!1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.052em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\!-\!1</annotation></semantics></math> such that, for every <math id="S4.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\in T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\in T</annotation></semantics></math>,
we have <math id="S4.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\cap C=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\cap C=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>, the projection <math id="S4.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> is decomposable,
and the general hyperplane <math id="S4.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> contains some <math id="S4.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\in T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\in T</annotation></semantics></math>.
We wish to apply Lemma <a href="#S4.Thmthm2" title="Lemma 4.2. ‣ 4. Families of decomposable subspaces ‣ Monodromy of Projective Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a> . For this, we
fix a line <math id="S4.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l\subset\mathbb{P}^{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>r</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l\subset\mathbb{P}^{r}</annotation></semantics></math> such that the open subset
<math id="S4.p11.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{l}=\{L\in T:L\cap l=\emptyset\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{l}=\{L\in T:L\cap l=\emptyset\}</annotation></semantics></math>
of <math id="S4.p11.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is nonempty. Setting <math id="S4.p11.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}=l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}=l</annotation></semantics></math>, we define</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:\widetilde{C}\times T_{l}\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}\times T_{l}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:\widetilde{C}\times T_{l}\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}\times T_{l}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p11.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p11.2.1" class="ltx_text">mapping a pair <math id="S4.p11.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(P,L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(P,L)</annotation></semantics></math> to the pair <math id="S4.p11.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\pi_{L}(P),L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\pi_{L}(P),L)</annotation></semantics></math> where by abuse of notation
<math id="S4.p11.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{L}(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{L}(P)</annotation></semantics></math> is the projection of <math id="S4.p11.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(P)</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S4.p11.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.p11.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text">By Lemma <a href="#S4.Thmthm2" title="Lemma 4.2. ‣ 4. Families of decomposable subspaces ‣ Monodromy of Projective Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a> we can find an open dense subset <math id="S4.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\subset T_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\subset T_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>,
a curve <math id="S4.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> and an integer <math id="S4.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\leq e\leq d/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\leq e\leq d/2</annotation></semantics></math>,
such that for every <math id="S4.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>
the morphism <math id="S4.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> factors as</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{C}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\alpha}}{{\rightarrow}}Y\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>α</mi></mover><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{C}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\alpha}}{{\rightarrow}}Y\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p12.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p12.2.1" class="ltx_text">where <math id="S4.p12.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> belongs to some irreducible component <math id="S4.p12.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>
of <math id="S4.p12.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mbox{Mor}_{e}(\widetilde{C},Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>Mor</mtext><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mbox{Mor}_{e}(\widetilde{C},Y)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text">Now we claim: if <math id="S4.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is a general hyperplane,
every degree <math id="S4.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> effective divisors <math id="S4.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\leq D_{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>H</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\leq D_{H}</annotation></semantics></math>
is a fibre of some morphism <math id="S4.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text">To prove the claim, we use the remark above that there is an open dense
subset <math id="S4.p14.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="A\subset\mathbb{P}^{r*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\subset\mathbb{P}^{r*}</annotation></semantics></math> such that for every hyperplane
<math id="S4.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\in A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\in A</annotation></semantics></math> we can find
<math id="S4.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.p14.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\subset H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\subset H</annotation></semantics></math>. We may also assume <math id="S4.p14.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is contained in the open
set <math id="S4.p14.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="U\!\subset\!\mathbb{P}^{r*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">⊂</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\!\subset\!\mathbb{P}^{r*}</annotation></semantics></math> of hyperplanes transverse to <math id="S4.p14.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>.
By Corollary <a href="#S4.Thmthm4" title="Corollary 4.4. ‣ 4. Families of decomposable subspaces ‣ Monodromy of Projective Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a> the set</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex27.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="I_{e}(A)=\{(E,H)\in\widetilde{C}_{e}\times U:\;E\leq D_{H},\;H\in A\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.558em">:</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{e}(A)=\{(E,H)\in\widetilde{C}_{e}\times U:\;E\leq D_{H},\;H\in A\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p14.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p14.2.1" class="ltx_text">is irreducible.
Let <math id="S4.p14.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\!\subset\!I_{e}(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\!\subset\!I_{e}(A)</annotation></semantics></math> be the subset consisting of those <math id="S4.p14.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(E,H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(E,H)</annotation></semantics></math>
for which the divisor <math id="S4.p14.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> is a fibre of some morphism <math id="S4.p14.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The projection <math id="S4.p14.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="pr_{2}:Z\rightarrow A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">pr_{2}:Z\rightarrow A</annotation></semantics></math> is surjective: indeed,
for every <math id="S4.p14.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\in A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\in A</annotation></semantics></math> we can pick <math id="S4.p14.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.p14.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> so that <math id="S4.p14.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\!\subset\!H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">⊂</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\!\subset\!H</annotation></semantics></math>, and
<math id="S4.p14.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> factors through some <math id="S4.p14.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, hence
<math id="S4.p14.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{H}=\pi_{L}^{-1}(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{H}=\pi_{L}^{-1}(H)</annotation></semantics></math> is union of fibres of <math id="S4.p14.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>.
In particular, <math id="S4.p14.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p14.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{e}(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{e}(A)</annotation></semantics></math> both have dimension <math id="S4.p14.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>, hence <math id="S4.p14.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> contains
an open dense subset <math id="S4.p14.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.p14.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{e}(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{e}(A)</annotation></semantics></math>. Since the projection <math id="S4.p14.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="pr_{2}:I_{e}(A)\rightarrow A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">pr_{2}:I_{e}(A)\rightarrow A</annotation></semantics></math>
is surjective and generically finite, we can find an open dense subset <math id="S4.p14.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{0}\!\subset\!A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">⊂</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{0}\!\subset\!A</annotation></semantics></math>
such that <math id="S4.p14.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="pr_{2}^{-1}(A_{0})\!\subset\!Z_{0}\!\subset\!Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">⊂</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.108em">⊂</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">pr_{2}^{-1}(A_{0})\!\subset\!Z_{0}\!\subset\!Z</annotation></semantics></math>, that is, for <math id="S4.p14.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\in A_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\in A_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>,
every degree <math id="S4.p14.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> effective divisors <math id="S4.p14.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\leq D_{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>H</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\leq D_{H}</annotation></semantics></math>
is a fibre of some morphism <math id="S4.p14.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the claim is proven.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p15.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p15.1.1" class="ltx_text">Next we show <math id="S4.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> is rational. By way of contradiction, suppose <math id="S4.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(Y)\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(Y)\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then <math id="S4.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mbox{Mor}_{e}(\widetilde{C},Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>Mor</mtext><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mbox{Mor}_{e}(\widetilde{C},Y)</annotation></semantics></math> is zero dimensional, so <math id="S4.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> consists of a unique
morphism <math id="S4.p15.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>. But then not every degree <math id="S4.p15.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> subdivisor of <math id="S4.p15.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{H}</annotation></semantics></math> can be a fibre of
<math id="S4.p15.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, hence <math id="S4.p15.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(Y)\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(Y)\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. On the other hand, if <math id="S4.p15.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(Y)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(Y)=1</annotation></semantics></math>, then two morphisms
in <math id="S4.p15.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> differ only by a translation of the elliptic curve <math id="S4.p15.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>.
In particular, the morphisms in <math id="S4.p15.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> have the same fibres, and as above
this is impossible. Thus <math id="S4.p15.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> must be rational.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p16.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p16.1.1" class="ltx_text">Finally we show <math id="S4.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is rational. We repeat the monodromy argument above,
replacing <math id="S4.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{e}(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{e}(A)</annotation></semantics></math> with the set</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex28" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex28.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="I_{e,e}(A)=\{(E_{1},E_{2},H)\in\widetilde{C}_{e}\times\widetilde{C}_{e}\times U:\;E_{1}+E_{2}\leq D_{H},\;H\in A\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.558em">:</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{e,e}(A)=\{(E_{1},E_{2},H)\in\widetilde{C}_{e}\times\widetilde{C}_{e}\times U:\;E_{1}+E_{2}\leq D_{H},\;H\in A\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p16.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p16.2.1" class="ltx_text">The conclusion is that for a general hyperplane <math id="S4.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> the following
holds: for every <math id="S4.p16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(E_{1},E_{2})\in\widetilde{C}_{e}\times\widetilde{C}_{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(E_{1},E_{2})\in\widetilde{C}_{e}\times\widetilde{C}_{e}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S4.p16.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{1}+E_{2}\leq D_{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>H</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{1}+E_{2}\leq D_{H}</annotation></semantics></math>
there is a subspace <math id="S4.p16.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.p16.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S4.p16.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\!\subset\!H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">⊂</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\!\subset\!H</annotation></semantics></math>, the morphism <math id="S4.p16.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{L}</annotation></semantics></math>
factors through a morphism <math id="S4.p16.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha:\widetilde{C}\rightarrow Y\cong\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>≅</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha:\widetilde{C}\rightarrow Y\cong\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>,
and <math id="S4.p16.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p16.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are fibres of <math id="S4.p16.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>.
In particular, any two such divisors <math id="S4.p16.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p16.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are linearly equivalent.
Now, if <math id="S4.p16.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p16.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>
are distinct points of <math id="S4.p16.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\cap H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\cap H</annotation></semantics></math>, we can find two divisors <math id="S4.p16.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p16.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> of degree <math id="S4.p16.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e-1</annotation></semantics></math>
such that <math id="S4.p16.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P+Q+F_{1}+F_{2}\leq D_{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>H</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P+Q+F_{1}+F_{2}\leq D_{H}</annotation></semantics></math>. Taking first <math id="S4.p16.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(E_{1},E_{2})=(P+F_{1},Q+F_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(E_{1},E_{2})=(P+F_{1},Q+F_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> and then
<math id="S4.p16.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(E_{1},E_{2})=(P+F_{2},Q+F_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(E_{1},E_{2})=(P+F_{2},Q+F_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> we see that <math id="S4.p16.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2P</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p16.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2Q</annotation></semantics></math> are linearly equivalent on <math id="S4.p16.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{C}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Now this holds for a general hyperplane <math id="S4.p16.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> and every pair of distinct points <math id="S4.p16.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p16.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>
in <math id="S4.p16.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\cap H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\cap H</annotation></semantics></math>, so we can find a point <math id="S4.p16.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{0}\in\widetilde{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{0}\in\widetilde{C}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S4.p16.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q-P_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q-P_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is a
2-torsion point in the Jacobian for infinitely many points <math id="S4.p16.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore <math id="S4.p16.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{C}</annotation></semantics></math> must be
rational.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 4.5</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the case of <math id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math>, the second hypothesis of Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmthm1" title="Theorem 4.1. ‣ 4. Families of decomposable subspaces ‣ Monodromy of Projective Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> means
<math id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is not a Schubert cycle <math id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(x)</annotation></semantics></math> consisting of the lines
through a point <math id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathbb{P}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathbb{P}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math>. Indeed, given a plane <math id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\subset\mathbb{P}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\subset\mathbb{P}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math>,
if <math id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(H)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the locus of lines contained
in <math id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>, it is well known that every irreducible
surface <math id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma\!\subset\!\mathbb{G}(1,\mathbb{P}^{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">⊂</mo><mrow><mi>𝔾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma\!\subset\!\mathbb{G}(1,\mathbb{P}^{3})</annotation></semantics></math> intersects <math id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(H)</annotation></semantics></math>
unless <math id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma=\sigma(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma=\sigma(x)</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathbb{P}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathbb{P}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 4.6</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\!\subset\!\mathbb{P}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\!\subset\!\mathbb{P}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> be a reduced irreducible nondegenerate curve.
Suppose <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is not rational, and <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma\!\subset\!\mathbb{G}(1,\mathbb{P}^{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">⊂</mo><mrow><mi>𝔾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma\!\subset\!\mathbb{G}(1,\mathbb{P}^{3})</annotation></semantics></math>
is an irreducible closed surface
whose general line does not meet <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> and is decomposable. Then</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex29" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma=\sigma(x)=\{L\in\mathbb{G}(1,\mathbb{P}^{3}):\&gt;\&gt;x\in L\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝔾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.718em">:</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma=\sigma(x)=\{L\in\mathbb{G}(1,\mathbb{P}^{3}):\&gt;\&gt;x\in L\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathbb{P}^{3}\!\smallsetminus\!C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo rspace="0.052em">∖</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathbb{P}^{3}\!\smallsetminus\!C</annotation></semantics></math> is one of the finitely many non-birational
points of <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.5" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p17.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p17.1.1" class="ltx_text">By Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmthm1" title="Theorem 4.1. ‣ 4. Families of decomposable subspaces ‣ Monodromy of Projective Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> and the previous remark, we know any such surface is
a Schubert cycle <math id="S4.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(x)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S4.p17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> is a point of <math id="S4.p17.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{3}\!\smallsetminus\!C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.052em" rspace="0.052em">∖</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{3}\!\smallsetminus\!C</annotation></semantics></math>. If projection
from <math id="S4.p17.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> were birational onto its image, then as in the proof of Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm6" title="Theorem 3.6. ‣ 3. The locus of non-uniform subspaces has codimension at least two ‣ Monodromy of Projective Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.6</span></a>
all but finitely many lines in <math id="S4.p17.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(x)</annotation></semantics></math> would be uniform, hence indecomposable.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5. </span>Surfaces of non-uniform lines for curves in <math id="S5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math></h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this paragraph we consider the case of <span id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">smooth</span> irreducible curves
<math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\!\subset\!\mathbb{P}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\!\subset\!\mathbb{P}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math>. As in Section <a href="#S3" title="3. The locus of non-uniform subspaces has codimension at least two ‣ Monodromy of Projective Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> we let
<math id="S5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}(C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(C)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the locus of lines that do not meet <math id="S5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> and are not uniform.
and we begin the classification of pairs
<math id="S5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(C,\Sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(C,\Sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is
an irreducible surface in <math id="S5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{G}(1,\mathbb{P}^{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{G}(1,\mathbb{P}^{3})</annotation></semantics></math> such that the
general line in <math id="S5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> belongs to <math id="S5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}(C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(C)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">By Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm1" title="Remark 3.1. ‣ 3. The locus of non-uniform subspaces has codimension at least two ‣ Monodromy of Projective Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> <math id="S5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}(C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(C)</annotation></semantics></math>
has dimension at most two, and we now wish to classify pairs
<math id="S5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(C,\Sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(C,\Sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is
an irreducible surface in <math id="S5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{G}(1,\mathbb{P}^{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{G}(1,\mathbb{P}^{3})</annotation></semantics></math> such that the
general line in <math id="S5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> belongs to <math id="S5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}(C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(C)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">First of all, as we observed in Remark <a href="#S3.Thmthm7" title="Remark 3.7. ‣ 3. The locus of non-uniform subspaces has codimension at least two ‣ Monodromy of Projective Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.7</span></a>,
if <math id="S5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathbb{P}^{3}\smallsetminus C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>∖</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathbb{P}^{3}\smallsetminus C</annotation></semantics></math> is one of the (finitely
many) points non birational points for <math id="S5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, then every line through <math id="S5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> is decomposable,
hence non-uniform. Thus, if <math id="S5.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(x)\cong\mathbb{P}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(x)\cong\mathbb{P}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the Schubert cycle
of lines through <math id="S5.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, for each
such point <math id="S5.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> the general line in <math id="S5.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(x)</annotation></semantics></math>
belongs to <math id="S5.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}(C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(C)</annotation></semantics></math>.
In fact we will show that, except <math id="S5.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is a rational curve of degree three, four or six,
these are the only irreducible surfaces in <math id="S5.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}(C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(C)</annotation></semantics></math>. To be more precise,
we make the following definition.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 5.1</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S5.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> be a nondegenerate irreducible curve in <math id="S5.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We say <math id="S5.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S5.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">has special monodromy</span> if there exists an irreducible Zariski closed
surface <math id="S5.Thmthm1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma\!\subset\!\mathbb{G}(1,\mathbb{P}^{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">⊂</mo><mrow><mi>𝔾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma\!\subset\!\mathbb{G}(1,\mathbb{P}^{3})</annotation></semantics></math> that is not a Schubert cycle <math id="S5.Thmthm1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(x)</annotation></semantics></math>
of lines through a point <math id="S5.Thmthm1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathbb{P}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathbb{P}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math>, with the property:
the general line <math id="S5.Thmthm1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\in\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\in\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> does not meet <math id="S5.Thmthm1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, and
the projection <math id="S5.Thmthm1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> factors as</p>
<table id="S5.Ex30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\alpha}}{{\rightarrow}}\mathbb{P}^{1}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\beta}}{{\rightarrow}}\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>α</mi></mover><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>β</mi></mover><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\alpha}}{{\rightarrow}}\mathbb{P}^{1}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\beta}}{{\rightarrow}}\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmthm1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S5.Thmthm1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> has degree <math id="S5.Thmthm1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We will show in Section <a href="#S6" title="6. Rational space curves admitting surfaces of decomposable lines ‣ Monodromy of Projective Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> that a curve with special monodromy is rational
of degree either <math id="S5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6</annotation></semantics></math>. In this section we prove:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 5.2</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> be a smooth nondegenerate irreducible curve in <math id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Suppose <math id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma\!\subset\!\mathbb{G}(1,\mathbb{P}^{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">⊂</mo><mrow><mi>𝔾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma\!\subset\!\mathbb{G}(1,\mathbb{P}^{3})</annotation></semantics></math> is an irreducible Zariski closed surface
such that the general line <math id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> does not meet <math id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> and is not uniform.
Then one of the following three possibilities holds:</span></p>
<ol id="S5.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">either there exists a non birational point </span><math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathbb{P}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathbb{P}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, and
</span><math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma=\sigma(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma=\sigma(x)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is the cycle of lines through </span><math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">; or</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a twisted cubic curve, and the general line in
</span><math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is the intersection of two osculating planes to </span><math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">; or</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is rational and has special monodromy.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 5.3</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The theorem is true more generally for an irreducible nondegenerate curve
<math id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\!\subset\!\mathbb{P}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\!\subset\!\mathbb{P}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> for which the branch divisor <math id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> of
Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmthm7" title="Proposition 2.7. ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Monodromy of Projective Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.7</span></a> is reduced.
In particular, it
holds for curves with only nodes or simple cusps. The proof is the same as below except
<math id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> has to be replaced by <math id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In the proof of the Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 5.2. ‣ 5. Surfaces of non-uniform lines for curves in 
        
          
            
            P
            3 ‣ Monodromy of Projective Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a> we will need the following two lemmas.
The first one is elementary and well known:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 5.4</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S5.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be a smooth irreducible projective curve.
Suppose <math id="S5.Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:X\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:X\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is a nonconstant morphism of degree <math id="S5.Thmthm4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>. Then
<math id="S5.Thmthm4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> ramifies over at least two distinct points <math id="S5.Thmthm4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.Thmthm4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S5.Thmthm4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
If there are no other
branch points, then <math id="S5.Thmthm4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is rational and up to a choice of coordinates <math id="S5.Thmthm4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>
is the map <math id="S5.Thmthm4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\mapsto z^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\mapsto z^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The second lemma we need can be proven using a result due to Strano:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 5.5</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Strano)<span id="S5.Thmthm5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\!\subset\!\mathbb{P}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\!\subset\!\mathbb{P}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> be an arbitrary reduced curve.
If <math id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma\!\subset\!\mathbb{G}(1,\mathbb{P}^{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">⊂</mo><mrow><mi>𝔾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma\!\subset\!\mathbb{G}(1,\mathbb{P}^{3})</annotation></semantics></math> is an irreducible Zariski closed surface
parametrizing lines that are trisecant to <math id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> (meaning <math id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\deg(X.L)\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg(X.L)\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>),
then one of the following holds:</span></p>
<ol id="S5.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S5.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S5.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">either </span><math id="S5.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma=\sigma(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma=\sigma(x)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I2.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, where </span><math id="S5.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I2.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a point in </span><math id="S5.I2.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I2.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> such that
</span><math id="S5.I2.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\deg_{x}(X.L)\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>deg</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg_{x}(X.L)\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I2.i1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for every line </span><math id="S5.I2.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I2.i1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> through </span><math id="S5.I2.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I2.i1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">; or</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S5.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma=\sigma(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma=\sigma(H)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,
where </span><math id="S5.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I2.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a plane in </span><math id="S5.I2.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I2.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> containing a subcurve </span><math id="S5.I2.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I2.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> of </span><math id="S5.I2.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I2.i2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">
of degree at least </span><math id="S5.I2.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I2.i2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, and </span><math id="S5.I2.i2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(H)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I2.i2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is the locus of lines
contained in </span><math id="S5.I2.i2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I2.i2.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">If the projection</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex31" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex31.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\rho:J_{\Sigma}=\{(H,L)\in\mathbb{P}^{3*}\times\Sigma:\;H\supset L\}\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{3*}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.558em">:</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>⊃</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho:J_{\Sigma}=\{(H,L)\in\mathbb{P}^{3*}\times\Sigma:\;H\supset L\}\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{3*}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p7.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p7.2.1" class="ltx_text">is not dominant, then by Remark <a href="#S4.Thmthm5" title="Remark 4.5. ‣ 4. Families of decomposable subspaces ‣ Monodromy of Projective Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a> there is a point <math id="S5.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathbb{P}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathbb{P}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="S5.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma=\sigma(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma=\sigma(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, and then every line through <math id="S5.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> meets <math id="S5.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> in a scheme of length at least
three. This is possible only if <math id="S5.p7.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\deg_{x}(X.L)\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>deg</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg_{x}(X.L)\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math> for every line <math id="S5.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\in\sigma(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\in\sigma(x)</annotation></semantics></math> .</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">Suppose now <math id="S5.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> is dominant. This means that the general plane
contains a trisecant line <math id="S5.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\in\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\in\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>. By <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>, Theorem 9,
<math id="S5.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> contains a unique subcurve <math id="S5.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=P(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=P(L)</annotation></semantics></math> whose general plane
section is <math id="S5.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L.X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L.X</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S5.p8.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\deg(L.X)\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg(L.X)\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is a plane curve
of degree at least <math id="S5.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S5.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(P)</annotation></semantics></math> be the linear span of <math id="S5.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>. Then
<math id="S5.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(H(P))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(H(P))</annotation></semantics></math> is a surface of trisecant lines for <math id="S5.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S5.p8.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>
contains finitely many subcurves and the general line <math id="S5.p8.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S5.p8.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is
contained in <math id="S5.p8.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(H(P(L)))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(H(P(L)))</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.p8.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> must be one of the Schubert
cycles <math id="S5.p8.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(H(P(L)))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(H(P(L)))</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 5.2. ‣ 5. Surfaces of non-uniform lines for curves in 
        
          
            
            P
            3 ‣ Monodromy of Projective Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S5.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">Suppose <math id="S5.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is a non-uniform line that does not meet <math id="S5.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then - see Remark <a href="#S3.Thmthm1" title="Remark 3.1. ‣ 3. The locus of non-uniform subspaces has codimension at least two ‣ Monodromy of Projective Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> - the line <math id="S5.p9.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}_{L}\subseteq\mathbb{P}^{3*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>⊆</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}_{L}\subseteq\mathbb{P}^{3*}</annotation></semantics></math>
dual to <math id="S5.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> meets the singular locus <math id="S5.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{*}_{sing}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{*}_{sing}</annotation></semantics></math> of the dual surface <math id="S5.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> in at
least two distinct points.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">Suppose first the dual of a
general line in <math id="S5.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> meets <math id="S5.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{*}_{sing}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{*}_{sing}</annotation></semantics></math> in more than two distinct points.
Let <math id="S5.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> denote the curve <math id="S5.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{*}_{sing}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{*}_{sing}</annotation></semantics></math> taken with its reduced scheme structure,
and apply Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmthm5" title="Lemma 5.5 (Strano). ‣ 5. Surfaces of non-uniform lines for curves in 
        
          
            
            P
            3 ‣ Monodromy of Projective Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.5</span></a> to <math id="S5.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>
and to the surface <math id="S5.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{\prime}=\{\mathbb{P}^{1}_{L}:L\in\Sigma\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{\prime}=\{\mathbb{P}^{1}_{L}:L\in\Sigma\}</annotation></semantics></math>. The conclusion is that either
there is a point <math id="S5.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S5.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{\prime}=\sigma(H^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{\prime}=\sigma(H^{*})</annotation></semantics></math>, or there
is a point <math id="S5.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathbb{P}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathbb{P}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S5.p10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma=\sigma(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma=\sigma(x)</annotation></semantics></math>.
We cannot have <math id="S5.p10.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{\prime}=\sigma(H^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{\prime}=\sigma(H^{*})</annotation></semantics></math>
because the general line through <math id="S5.p10.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S5.p10.m13" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{3*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{3*}</annotation></semantics></math> meets the curve <math id="S5.p10.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> only at
<math id="S5.p10.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, while we are assuming that a general line in <math id="S5.p10.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> meets <math id="S5.p10.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> in more than two points.
Thus <math id="S5.p10.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma=\sigma(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma=\sigma(x)</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore, as in the proof of <a href="#S4.Thmthm6" title="Corollary 4.6. ‣ 4. Families of decomposable subspaces ‣ Monodromy of Projective Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.6</span></a>,
the point <math id="S5.p10.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> is not birational.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">Suppose now a general line <math id="S5.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S5.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> meets <math id="S5.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> - the singular locus of <math id="S5.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>-
in exactly two points <math id="S5.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{2}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{2}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.
If <math id="S5.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> meets the dual surface <math id="S5.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> only at <math id="S5.p11.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p11.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{2}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{2}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>,
the branch divisor of <math id="S5.p11.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> is supported at <math id="S5.p11.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p11.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{2}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{2}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then,
by Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmthm4" title="Lemma 5.4. ‣ 5. Surfaces of non-uniform lines for curves in 
        
          
            
            P
            3 ‣ Monodromy of Projective Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.4</span></a>, the curve
<math id="S5.p11.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is rational
and <math id="S5.p11.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{L}:C\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{L}:C\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is given in some coordinates by <math id="S5.p11.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\mapsto z^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\mapsto z^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus <math id="S5.p11.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is the intersection
of two planes <math id="S5.p11.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p11.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> each of them
meeting <math id="S5.p11.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> at a single point with multiplicity <math id="S5.p11.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>. As <math id="S5.p11.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> varies in a surface, there must
be infinitely many such osculating planes, and this is possible (since we are in char. zero)
only if <math id="S5.p11.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=3</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus <math id="S5.p11.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is a twisted cubic curve, and
<math id="S5.p11.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> consists of those lines that are intersection of two osculating planes to <math id="S5.p11.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text">We are left with the case when, for a general <math id="S5.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S5.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>, the line <math id="S5.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}_{L}</annotation></semantics></math>
meets <math id="S5.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{*}_{sing}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{*}_{sing}</annotation></semantics></math> in exactly two points <math id="S5.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{2}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{2}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, but it also
contains a smooth point <math id="S5.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}_{3}\in C^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mn>3</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}_{3}\in C^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, by <a href="#S2.Thmthm7" title="Proposition 2.7. ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Monodromy of Projective Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.7</span></a> and <a href="#S2.Thmthm9" title="Proposition 2.9. ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Monodromy of Projective Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.9</span></a>, the monodromy group of
<math id="S5.p12.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{L}:\widetilde{C}\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{L}:\widetilde{C}\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> contains a
transposition. Therefore by <a href="#S2.Thmthm2" title="Remark 2.2. ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Monodromy of Projective Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>
the map <math id="S5.p12.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> factors nontrivially
as
<math id="S5.p12.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{C}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\alpha}}{{\rightarrow}}Y\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\beta}}{{\rightarrow}}\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>α</mi></mover><mi>Y</mi><mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>β</mi></mover><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{C}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\alpha}}{{\rightarrow}}Y\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\beta}}{{\rightarrow}}\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
If the map <math id="S5.p12.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> ramified over more than two distinct points
of <math id="S5.p12.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}_{L}</annotation></semantics></math>, these points would all be singular points of <math id="S5.p12.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>
by <a href="#S2.Thmthm7" title="Proposition 2.7. ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Monodromy of Projective Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.7</span></a> because <math id="S5.p12.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg(\alpha)\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg(\alpha)\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, contradicting the assumption
that <math id="S5.p12.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> meet <math id="S5.p12.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{*}_{sing}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{*}_{sing}</annotation></semantics></math> in exactly two distinct points.
Now Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmthm4" title="Lemma 5.4. ‣ 5. Surfaces of non-uniform lines for curves in 
        
          
            
            P
            3 ‣ Monodromy of Projective Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.4</span></a> implies <math id="S5.p12.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\cong\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>≅</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\cong\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is rational, and up to a choice of
coordinates
<math id="S5.p12.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta:\mathbb{P}^{1}\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta:\mathbb{P}^{1}\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is the map <math id="S5.p12.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\mapsto z^{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>e</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\mapsto z^{e}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S5.p12.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e=\deg(\beta)\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e=\deg(\beta)\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>.
We cannot have <math id="S5.p12.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math> because as above this would imply the existence
of infinitely many hyperosculating planes to <math id="S5.p12.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>. We claim the case <math id="S5.p12.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e=3</annotation></semantics></math> is also impossible.
Since <math id="S5.p12.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg(\alpha)\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg(\alpha)\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, if we had <math id="S5.p12.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e=3</annotation></semantics></math>, we would have either infinitely many
planes that osculate <math id="S5.p12.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> at more than one point, or infinitely many hyperosculating
planes. But <math id="S5.p12.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> has only finitely many hyperosculating planes, while
the first alternative contradicts the fact that the
map sending a point <math id="S5.p12.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\in\widetilde{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∈</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\in\widetilde{C}</annotation></semantics></math> to the osculating plane at <math id="S5.p12.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>
is birational.
Thus <math id="S5.p12.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e=\deg(\beta)=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e=\deg(\beta)=2</annotation></semantics></math>.
Finally, if <math id="S5.p12.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is not a cycle <math id="S5.p12.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, the curve <math id="S5.p12.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> must be rational
by Corollary <a href="#S4.Thmthm6" title="Corollary 4.6. ‣ 4. Families of decomposable subspaces ‣ Monodromy of Projective Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.6</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6. </span>Rational space curves admitting surfaces of decomposable lines</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section we classify smooth curves in <math id="S6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> having special
monodromy. The main result is that such a curve is either a
rational quartic or a rational sextic.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We begin with some preliminary remarks. Suppose <math id="S6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\!\subset\!\mathbb{P}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\!\subset\!\mathbb{P}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> is a degree <math id="S6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2d\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2d\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math>
smooth rational curve with special monodromy. Recall this means
there exists an irreducible two dimensional family
of lines <math id="S6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>, not contained in a Schubert cycle <math id="S6.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, with the property:
every line <math id="S6.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\in\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\in\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> in the family does not meet <math id="S6.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, and
the projection <math id="S6.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> factors as</p>
<table id="S6.Ex32" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\alpha_{L}}}{{\rightarrow}}\mathbb{P}^{1}_{L}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\beta_{L}}}{{\rightarrow}}\mathbb{P}^{1}_{L}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></mover><msubsup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></mover><msubsup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\alpha_{L}}}{{\rightarrow}}\mathbb{P}^{1}_{L}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\beta_{L}}}{{\rightarrow}}\mathbb{P}^{1}_{L}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S6.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> has degree <math id="S6.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">As above, we denote by <math id="S6.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\subset\mbox{H}^{0}(\mathbb{P}^{1},{\mathcal{O}}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}(2d))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\subset\mbox{H}^{0}(\mathbb{P}^{1},{\mathcal{O}}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}(2d))</annotation></semantics></math> the <math id="S6.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional subspace defining the embedding
<math id="S6.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\!\subset\!\mathbb{P}^{3}=\mathbb{P}(V^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\!\subset\!\mathbb{P}^{3}=\mathbb{P}(V^{*})</annotation></semantics></math>. Since the morphisms <math id="S6.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> have
degree two, the vector space <math id="S6.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> contains a lot of forms <math id="S6.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>
with <math id="S6.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\in\mbox{H}^{0}(\mathbb{P}^{1},{\mathcal{O}}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}(d))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\in\mbox{H}^{0}(\mathbb{P}^{1},{\mathcal{O}}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}(d))</annotation></semantics></math>. To make this
precise, from now on we let <math id="S6.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}_{m}=\mathbb{P}(\mbox{H}^{0}(\mathbb{P}^{1},{\mathcal{O}}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}(m)))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}_{m}=\mathbb{P}(\mbox{H}^{0}(\mathbb{P}^{1},{\mathcal{O}}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}(m)))</annotation></semantics></math> and look at the embedding <math id="S6.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q:\mathbb{P}_{d}\rightarrow\mathbb{P}_{2d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q:\mathbb{P}_{d}\rightarrow\mathbb{P}_{2d}</annotation></semantics></math> that sends (the class of) a degree <math id="S6.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> form to (the
class of) its square. We will refer to <math id="S6.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> or to its image
<math id="S6.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\!\subset\!\mathbb{P}_{2d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">⊂</mo><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\!\subset\!\mathbb{P}_{2d}</annotation></semantics></math> as the <span id="S6.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">quadratic Veronese</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmthm1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 6.1</span></span><span id="S6.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S6.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\!\subset\!\mathbb{P}(V^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">⊂</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\!\subset\!\mathbb{P}(V^{*})</annotation></semantics></math> be a smooth rational
curve of degree <math id="S6.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2d\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2d\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math> with special monodromy. Then the
intersection <math id="S6.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\cap\mathbb{P}(V)\subset\mathbb{P}_{2d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\cap\mathbb{P}(V)\subset\mathbb{P}_{2d}</annotation></semantics></math> has dimension at
least one.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S6.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">By definition of special monodromy, the curve <math id="S6.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> admits
a two dimensional family <math id="S6.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma\subset\mathbb{G}(1,\mathbb{P}(V^{*}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>𝔾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma\subset\mathbb{G}(1,\mathbb{P}(V^{*}))</annotation></semantics></math> of decomposable
lines such that, for every <math id="S6.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\in\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\in\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>,
the projection <math id="S6.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> factors through a degree two morphism
<math id="S6.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{L}:\mathbb{P}^{1}\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{L}:\mathbb{P}^{1}\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}_{L}</annotation></semantics></math>. Up to a choice of coordinates,
<math id="S6.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> is the map <math id="S6.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\mapsto z^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\mapsto z^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, hence the line <math id="S6.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}_{L}\subset\mathbb{P}(V)\subset\mathbb{P}_{2d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}_{L}\subset\mathbb{P}(V)\subset\mathbb{P}_{2d}</annotation></semantics></math> intersects <math id="S6.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> in at least two
points. Thus the surface</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex33" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{\prime}=\{\mathbb{P}^{1}_{L}\in\mathbb{G}(1,\mathbb{P}(V)):L\in\Sigma\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝔾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{\prime}=\{\mathbb{P}^{1}_{L}\in\mathbb{G}(1,\mathbb{P}(V)):L\in\Sigma\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">is contained in the set of lines secant to <math id="S6.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\cap\mathbb{P}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\cap\mathbb{P}(V)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Hence <math id="S6.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\cap\mathbb{P}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\cap\mathbb{P}(V)</annotation></semantics></math> is at least one dimensional.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Thus, if <math id="S6.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> has special monodromy, we can find a curve <math id="S6.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\subset\mathbb{P}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\subset\mathbb{P}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S6.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(Y)</annotation></semantics></math> lies in the three dimensional subspace
<math id="S6.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(V)</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S6.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}_{2d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}_{2d}</annotation></semantics></math>, and one expects there are very few such
curves. In fact, we show below that <math id="S6.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> must either be a conic in
<math id="S6.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> or a twisted cubic in <math id="S6.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}_{3}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Our first remark is that, since <math id="S6.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth, we can exclude the
case <math id="S6.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> is a line:</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmthm2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 6.2</span></span><span id="S6.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S6.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\!\subset\!\mathbb{P}(V^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">⊂</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\!\subset\!\mathbb{P}(V^{*})</annotation></semantics></math> be
a smooth rational curve of degree <math id="S6.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2d\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2d\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math>. For any line <math id="S6.Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\!\subset\!\mathbb{P}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">⊂</mo><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\!\subset\!\mathbb{P}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> the conic <math id="S6.Thmthm2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(Y)</annotation></semantics></math> is not contained in <math id="S6.Thmthm2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(V)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S6.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">The linear span of the conic <math id="S6.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(Y)</annotation></semantics></math> is a plane <math id="S6.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(W)\!\subset\!\mathbb{P}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">⊂</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(W)\!\subset\!\mathbb{P}(V)</annotation></semantics></math>,
and the linear series <math id="S6.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(W)</annotation></semantics></math> defines a projection
<math id="S6.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{2}=\mathbb{P}(W^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{2}=\mathbb{P}(W^{*})</annotation></semantics></math> whose image is a conic.
Thus <math id="S6.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is contained in a quadric cone.
But on the quadric cone there are no smooth rational curves of degree greater than <math id="S6.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>,
for example by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, Exercise 2.9 p. 384]</cite>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p8.1" class="ltx_p">We can also say that <math id="S6.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(Y)</annotation></semantics></math> spans <math id="S6.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(V)</annotation></semantics></math>. One way to prove this
is to observe:</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmthm3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 6.3</span></span><span id="S6.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The quadratic Veronese
<math id="S6.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=q(\mathbb{P}_{d})\!\subset\!\mathbb{P}_{2d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">⊂</mo><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=q(\mathbb{P}_{d})\!\subset\!\mathbb{P}_{2d}</annotation></semantics></math> contains no four collinear
points.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S6.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">Fix a line <math id="S6.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\!\subset\!\mathbb{P}_{2d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">⊂</mo><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\!\subset\!\mathbb{P}_{2d}</annotation></semantics></math>, and think of it as a linear
series <math id="S6.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{1}_{2d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{1}_{2d}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S6.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose <math id="S6.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> contains two distinct
points of <math id="S6.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S6.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to a subspace <math id="S6.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle g^{2},h^{2}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle g^{2},h^{2}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S6.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mbox{H}^{0}(\mathbb{P}^{1},{\mathcal{O}}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}(d))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mbox{H}^{0}(\mathbb{P}^{1},{\mathcal{O}}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}(d))</annotation></semantics></math>. It follows
that, if <math id="S6.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> has a base point <math id="S6.p9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, then in fact <math id="S6.p9.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2p</annotation></semantics></math> is
in the base locus of <math id="S6.p9.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>. We can then remove <math id="S6.p9.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2p</annotation></semantics></math> from the base
locus and deduce the statement from the case <math id="S6.p9.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\!-\!1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.052em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\!-\!1</annotation></semantics></math> by
induction. Therefore we can assume <math id="S6.p9.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is base point free and
defines a morphism <math id="S6.p9.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:\mathbb{P}^{1}\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:\mathbb{P}^{1}\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> of degree <math id="S6.p9.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2d</annotation></semantics></math>. By
Riemann-Hurwitz the ramification divisor has degree</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex34" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg(R_{\pi})=4d-2." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg(R_{\pi})=4d-2.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p9.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p9.2.1" class="ltx_text">Now, if the linear series <math id="S6.p9.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> contains a divisor <math id="S6.p9.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2D</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S6.p9.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\!\subset\!R_{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">⊂</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>π</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\!\subset\!R_{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S6.p9.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> has degree <math id="S6.p9.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, there are at most
<math id="S6.p9.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> such divisors in <math id="S6.p9.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, that is, the support of <math id="S6.p9.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\cap M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\cap M</annotation></semantics></math>
consists of at most <math id="S6.p9.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> points (by contrast, <math id="S6.p9.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> can be tangent to
<math id="S6.p9.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> at one or more points of intersection, and the scheme
theoretic intersection <math id="S6.p9.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\cap M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\cap M</annotation></semantics></math> may well have length <math id="S6.p9.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>).
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmthm4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 6.4</span></span><span id="S6.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmthm4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S6.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\!\subset\!\mathbb{P}(V^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">⊂</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\!\subset\!\mathbb{P}(V^{*})</annotation></semantics></math> be a smooth rational
curve of degree <math id="S6.Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2d\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2d\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose <math id="S6.Thmthm4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\subset\mathbb{P}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\subset\mathbb{P}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is a curve
such that <math id="S6.Thmthm4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(Y)\subset\mathbb{P}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(Y)\subset\mathbb{P}(V)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S6.Thmthm4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(V)</annotation></semantics></math> is the linear span
of <math id="S6.Thmthm4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(Y)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S6.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S6.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=q(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=q(Y)</annotation></semantics></math>, and note <math id="S6.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is a curve in <math id="S6.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\cap\mathbb{P}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\cap\mathbb{P}(V)</annotation></semantics></math>. Since
<math id="S6.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=q(\mathbb{P}_{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=q(\mathbb{P}_{d})</annotation></semantics></math> is an embedding of <math id="S6.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> defined by a linear system
of quadrics, every curve in <math id="S6.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> - and in particular <math id="S6.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> - has even
degree. Now <math id="S6.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is not a conic by Lemma <a href="#S6.Thmthm2" title="Lemma 6.2. ‣ 6. Rational space curves admitting surfaces of decomposable lines ‣ Monodromy of Projective Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.2</span></a>, and it cannot
be a plane curve of degree <math id="S6.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math> because <math id="S6.p10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> contains no <math id="S6.p10.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>
collinear points. Thus <math id="S6.p10.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> spans <math id="S6.p10.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(V)</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Before proving <math id="S6.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> must be a conic or a twisted cubic, we recall
results due as far as we know to Hopf and
Eisenbud <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, 5.2]</cite>. We denote the linear span of a
subset <math id="S6.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\!\subset\!\mathbb{P}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\!\subset\!\mathbb{P}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> by the symbol <math id="S6.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle S\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle S\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmthm5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 6.5</span></span><span id="S6.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmthm5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S6.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q:\mathbb{P}_{d}\rightarrow\mathbb{P}_{2d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q:\mathbb{P}_{d}\rightarrow\mathbb{P}_{2d}</annotation></semantics></math> be the quadratic
Veronese.</span></p>
<ol id="S6.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S6.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(Hopf)
If </span><math id="S6.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\!\subset\!\mathbb{P}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">⊂</mo><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\!\subset\!\mathbb{P}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a linear subspace of dimension </span><math id="S6.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,
then</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex35" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim\,\langle q(H)\rangle\geq 2r." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim\,\langle q(H)\rangle\geq 2r.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(Eisenbud)
Equality holds if and only if there exist a two dimensional
subspace </span><math id="S6.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\!\subset\!\mathbb{P}_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">⊂</mo><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\!\subset\!\mathbb{P}_{a}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and a form </span><math id="S6.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\in\mathbb{P}_{d-ra}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\in\mathbb{P}_{d-ra}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> such
that </span><math id="S6.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H=\mathbb{P}(h\mbox{S}^{r}(W))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">S</mtext><mi>r</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H=\mathbb{P}(h\mbox{S}^{r}(W))</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.I1.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> where </span><math id="S6.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mbox{S}^{r}(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">S</mtext><mi>r</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mbox{S}^{r}(W)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.I1.i2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is the
image of </span><math id="S6.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mbox{Sym}^{r}(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Sym</mtext><mi>r</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mbox{Sym}^{r}(W)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.I1.i2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> in the space of forms of degree
</span><math id="S6.I1.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="ra" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ra</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.I1.i2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> - that is, there exist two linearly independent forms </span><math id="S6.I1.i2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.I1.i2.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and
</span><math id="S6.I1.i2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.I1.i2.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> of degree </span><math id="S6.I1.i2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.I1.i2.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> such that </span><math id="S6.I1.i2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H=\mathbb{P}(\langle hF^{r},hF^{r-1}G,\ldots,hG^{r}\rangle)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>r</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>r</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H=\mathbb{P}(\langle hF^{r},hF^{r-1}G,\ldots,hG^{r}\rangle)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.I1.i2.p1.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmthm6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 6.6</span></span><span id="S6.Thmthm6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmthm6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmthm6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose <math id="S6.Thmthm6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\!\subset\!\mathbb{P}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">⊂</mo><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\!\subset\!\mathbb{P}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is a reduced
irreducible curve of degree <math id="S6.Thmthm6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S6.Thmthm6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(Y)</annotation></semantics></math> spans a three
dimensional linear subspace <math id="S6.Thmthm6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(V)</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S6.Thmthm6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}_{2d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}_{2d}</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose
further the linear series <math id="S6.Thmthm6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(V)</annotation></semantics></math> is base point free and defines
an embedding <math id="S6.Thmthm6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\mathbb{P}^{1}\rightarrow\mathbb{P}(V^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\mathbb{P}^{1}\rightarrow\mathbb{P}(V^{*})</annotation></semantics></math> with image <math id="S6.Thmthm6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>. Then either
<math id="S6.Thmthm6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=e=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=e=2</annotation></semantics></math>, or <math id="S6.Thmthm6.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=e=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=e=3</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.Thmthm6.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> is a twisted cubic. In particular, <math id="S6.Thmthm6.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>
is either a rational quartic or a rational sextic.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.5" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S6.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text">Given a point <math id="S6.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, we denote by <math id="S6.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(s)\!\subset\!\mathbb{P}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">⊂</mo><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(s)\!\subset\!\mathbb{P}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>
the hyperplane of forms vanishing on <math id="S6.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that by assumption
<math id="S6.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(V)</annotation></semantics></math> is base point free, hence <math id="S6.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> is not contained in a
hyperplane <math id="S6.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(s)</annotation></semantics></math>. We first prove the following claim:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For general </span><math id="S6.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.p13.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, the intersection
of </span><math id="S6.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(s)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.p13.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S6.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.p13.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is transverse, i.e., consists of </span><math id="S6.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.p13.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> distinct points.
</span><span id="S6.p13.1.6" class="ltx_text"></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S6.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> has only finitely many singular points and each of them is contained
in only finitely many hyperplanes of the form <math id="S6.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(s)</annotation></semantics></math>, the general hyperplane
<math id="S6.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(s)</annotation></semantics></math> does not meet the singular locus of <math id="S6.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus if
the claim were false, for every <math id="S6.p14.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S6.p14.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> the hyperplane <math id="S6.p14.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(s)</annotation></semantics></math> would be tangent to
<math id="S6.p14.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>. We contend this implies <math id="S6.p14.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\!\subset\!\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">⊂</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\!\subset\!\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S6.p14.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> is the
hypersurface of polynomials having a double root.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p15.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p15.1.1" class="ltx_text">Analytic proof: consider the normalization morphism <math id="S6.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g:\widetilde{Y}\rightarrow Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g:\widetilde{Y}\rightarrow Y</annotation></semantics></math>
and the correspondence</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex36" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J=\{(x,s)\in\widetilde{Y}\times\mathbb{P}^{1}:\,\,T_{x}Y\!\subset\!H(s)\;\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.618em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">⊂</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J=\{(x,s)\in\widetilde{Y}\times\mathbb{P}^{1}:\,\,T_{x}Y\!\subset\!H(s)\;\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p15.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p15.2.1" class="ltx_text">We are assuming the second projection <math id="S6.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is surjective. Since
<math id="S6.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> is one dimensional, we can find near a general point <math id="S6.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>
a local analytic section <math id="S6.p15.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\mapsto(x(s),s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\mapsto(x(s),s)</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S6.p15.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{2}:J\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{2}:J\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Choose
local coordinates <math id="S6.p15.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S6.p15.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p15.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S6.p15.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and write <math id="S6.p15.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(x,s)\in\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(x,s)\in\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>
for the value of the polynomial <math id="S6.p15.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(x)</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S6.p15.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>. The condition <math id="S6.p15.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{x(s)}Y\!\subset\!H(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">⊂</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{x(s)}Y\!\subset\!H(s)</annotation></semantics></math>
means <math id="S6.p15.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(x(s),s)=\partial_{x}g(x(s),s)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(x(s),s)=\partial_{x}g(x(s),s)=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex37" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0=\frac{d}{ds}\,g(x(s),s)=\partial_{x}g(x(s),s)x^{\prime}(s)+\partial_{s}g(x(s),s)=\partial_{s}g(x(s),s)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>s</mi></msub><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>s</mi></msub><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0=\frac{d}{ds}\,g(x(s),s)=\partial_{x}g(x(s),s)x^{\prime}(s)+\partial_{s}g(x(s),s)=\partial_{s}g(x(s),s)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p15.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p15.3.1" class="ltx_text">so that <math id="S6.p15.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> is a double root of the polynomial <math id="S6.p15.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(x(s))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(x(s))</annotation></semantics></math>. It follows that
<math id="S6.p15.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\!\subset\!\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">⊂</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\!\subset\!\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p16.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p16.1.1" class="ltx_text">Geometric proof: to say for general <math id="S6.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> the hyperplane <math id="S6.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(s)</annotation></semantics></math> is tangent
to <math id="S6.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> is to say the hypersurface <math id="S6.p16.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="Y^{*}\!\subset\!\mathbb{P}^{d*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.108em">⊂</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y^{*}\!\subset\!\mathbb{P}^{d*}</annotation></semantics></math> dual to <math id="S6.p16.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>
contains the rational normal curve</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex38" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma=\{H(s):\,s\in\mathbb{P}^{1}\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.448em">:</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma=\{H(s):\,s\in\mathbb{P}^{1}\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S6.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p17.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p17.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S6.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma\!\subset\!Y^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">⊂</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma\!\subset\!Y^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, a tangent line <math id="S6.p17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S6.p17.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is contained in
some hyperplane <math id="S6.p17.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> tangent to <math id="S6.p17.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., belonging to <math id="S6.p17.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y^{**}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mo>∗</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>∗</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y^{**}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then <math id="S6.p17.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{L}\in Y^{**}\cap\Gamma^{*}=Y\cap\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mo>∗</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>∗</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∩</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{L}\in Y^{**}\cap\Gamma^{*}=Y\cap\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>, where the last
equality follows from the biduality theorem and the fact that <math id="S6.p17.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is the dual
of <math id="S6.p17.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>. Now <math id="S6.p17.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> has to vary in a one
dimensional family because <math id="S6.p17.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is nondegenerate. Hence <math id="S6.p17.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\cap\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\cap\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>
is one dimensional, and therefore must coincide with <math id="S6.p17.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p18.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p18.1.1" class="ltx_text">We have shown <math id="S6.p18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\!\subset\!\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">⊂</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\!\subset\!\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>, that is, every form in <math id="S6.p18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> has at least one
root of multiplicity at least <math id="S6.p18.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>. It follows the linear series <math id="S6.p18.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(V)</annotation></semantics></math> spanned
by <math id="S6.p18.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(Y)</annotation></semantics></math> contains infinitely many divisors with multiplicity at least <math id="S6.p18.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> at one
point. But, since <math id="S6.p18.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(V)</annotation></semantics></math> is three dimensional, this implies the linear series has
infinitely many inflectionary points, a contradiction. Therefore the claim holds:
for <math id="S6.p18.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> general in <math id="S6.p18.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> the hyperplane <math id="S6.p18.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(s)</annotation></semantics></math> meets <math id="S6.p18.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S6.p18.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e=\deg(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e=\deg(Y)</annotation></semantics></math> distinct
points.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p19" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p19.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p19.1.1" class="ltx_text">We now want to apply Riemann-Hurwitz to a general tangential projection of <math id="S6.p19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>
and use the claim to prove that, if <math id="S6.p19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> is not small, then the ramification divisor
is too large. By a theorem of Kaji <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>, for <math id="S6.p19.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> general in <math id="S6.p19.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>,
the tangent line <math id="S6.p19.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{f(s)}C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{f(s)}C</annotation></semantics></math>
meets <math id="S6.p19.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> only at <math id="S6.p19.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(s)</annotation></semantics></math> and with multiplicity two.
Hence projection from <math id="S6.p19.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{f(s)}C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{f(s)}C</annotation></semantics></math> is a degree <math id="S6.p19.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2d-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2d-2</annotation></semantics></math> morphism
<math id="S6.p19.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:C=\mathbb{P}^{1}\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:C=\mathbb{P}^{1}\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>
In other words, <math id="S6.p19.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> is the morphism defined by the base point free <math id="S6.p19.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{1}_{2d-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{1}_{2d-2}</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex39" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex39.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{D-2s:\&gt;D\in|V|,D\geq 2s\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.498em">:</mo><mi>D</mi><mo rspace="0em">∈</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>V</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{D-2s:\&gt;D\in|V|,D\geq 2s\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p19.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p19.2.1" class="ltx_text">Now let <math id="S6.p19.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> be a form in <math id="S6.p19.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\cap H(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\cap H(s)</annotation></semantics></math>: then <math id="S6.p19.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2s</annotation></semantics></math> is a double root
of <math id="S6.p19.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{2}\in\mathbb{P}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{2}\in\mathbb{P}(V)</annotation></semantics></math>, hence our <math id="S6.p19.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{1}_{2d-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{1}_{2d-2}</annotation></semantics></math> contains the divisor</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex40" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex40.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g^{2})_{0}-2s=2s_{1}+\cdots+2s_{d-1}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g^{2})_{0}-2s=2s_{1}+\cdots+2s_{d-1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p19.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p19.3.1" class="ltx_text">and we see <math id="S6.p19.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{1}+\cdots+s_{d-1}\leq R_{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>π</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{1}+\cdots+s_{d-1}\leq R_{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S6.p19.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math> is the ramification
divisor of <math id="S6.p19.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S6.p19.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\cap H(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\cap H(s)</annotation></semantics></math> consists of <math id="S6.p19.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> distinct points, we conclude
<math id="S6.p19.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg R_{\pi}\geq e(d-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>π</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg R_{\pi}\geq e(d-1)</annotation></semantics></math>. By Riemann-Hurwitz then</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex41" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex41.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-2=-2(2d-2)+\deg(R_{\pi})\geq-2(2d-2)+e(d-1)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0em">≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-2=-2(2d-2)+\deg(R_{\pi})\geq-2(2d-2)+e(d-1)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p19.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p19.4.1" class="ltx_text">so that</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex42" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex42.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e\leq 4-\frac{2}{d-1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e\leq 4-\frac{2}{d-1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p19.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p19.5.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S6.p19.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> (otherwise the span of <math id="S6.p19.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(Y)</annotation></semantics></math> would be a <math id="S6.p19.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>), we must have
either <math id="S6.p19.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e=3</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p19.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S6.p19.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e=2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p19.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p20" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p20.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p20.1.1" class="ltx_text">Suppose first <math id="S6.p20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e=2</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S6.p20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> is a smooth conic spanning a
2-dimensional linear subspace <math id="S6.p20.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\subseteq\mathbb{P}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\subseteq\mathbb{P}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S6.p20.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="q_{|H}:H\rightarrow\mathbb{P}_{2d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{|H}:H\rightarrow\mathbb{P}_{2d}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by a linear system of quadrics, the
linear space <math id="S6.p20.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle q(H)\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle q(H)\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> has dimension at most <math id="S6.p20.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math>, and
by Hopf at least <math id="S6.p20.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>. If it has dimension <math id="S6.p20.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S6.p20.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="q_{|H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{|H}</annotation></semantics></math> is
the Veronese embedding of <math id="S6.p20.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H=\mathbb{P}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H=\mathbb{P}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and then <math id="S6.p20.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> maps any conic
of <math id="S6.p20.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> to a hyperplane section of the Veronese surface, and so
<math id="S6.p20.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(Y)</annotation></semantics></math> spans a <math id="S6.p20.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math>, contrary to our assumptions. Hence
<math id="S6.p20.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle q(H)\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle q(H)\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S6.p20.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p21" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p21.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p21.1.1" class="ltx_text">Now by Eisenbud’s result <a href="#S6.Thmthm5" title="Proposition 6.5. ‣ 6. Rational space curves admitting surfaces of decomposable lines ‣ Monodromy of Projective Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.5</span></a>(2) we have</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex43" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex43.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle q(H)\rangle=\mathbb{P}(\langle h^{2}F^{4},h^{2}F^{3}G,h^{2}F^{2}G^{2},h^{2}FG^{3},h^{2}G^{4}\rangle)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle q(H)\rangle=\mathbb{P}(\langle h^{2}F^{4},h^{2}F^{3}G,h^{2}F^{2}G^{2},h^{2}FG^{3},h^{2}G^{4}\rangle).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p21.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p21.2.1" class="ltx_text">Thus the linear series <math id="S6.p21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle q(H)\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle q(H)\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> defines a morphism <math id="S6.p21.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> whose
image is a normal rational quartic <math id="S6.p21.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, and the linear subseries <math id="S6.p21.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(V)</annotation></semantics></math> maps <math id="S6.p21.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> to a
projection <math id="S6.p21.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S6.p21.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S6.p21.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(V^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(V^{*})</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore <math id="S6.p21.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg(C)\leq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg(C)\leq 4</annotation></semantics></math>, that is, <math id="S6.p21.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=2</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p22" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p22.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p22.1.1" class="ltx_text">Finally suppose <math id="S6.p22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e=3</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p22.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>. First observe <math id="S6.p22.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> is a
twisted cubic curve. Otherwise <math id="S6.p22.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> would be a plane cubic and
would have infinitely many trisecant lines. This is impossible
since <math id="S6.p22.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\!\subset\!q^{-1}(\mathbb{P}(V))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\!\subset\!q^{-1}(\mathbb{P}(V))</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p22.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{-1}(\mathbb{P}(V))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{-1}(\mathbb{P}(V))</annotation></semantics></math> is cut
out by quadrics. Thus <math id="S6.p22.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> is a twisted cubic curve spanning a
three dimensional subspace <math id="S6.p22.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\!\subset\!\mathbb{P}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">⊂</mo><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\!\subset\!\mathbb{P}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S6.p22.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{s}=\;\langle q(H)\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{s}=\;\langle q(H)\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>. By hypothesis <math id="S6.p22.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(Y)</annotation></semantics></math> spans a <math id="S6.p22.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> in
<math id="S6.p22.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{s}</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="S6.p22.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> is contained in <math id="S6.p22.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s-3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s-3</annotation></semantics></math> linearly independent
quadrics. But <math id="S6.p22.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> is a twisted cubic curve, thus <math id="S6.p22.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{0}(H,{\mathcal{I}}_{Y,H}(2))=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{0}(H,{\mathcal{I}}_{Y,H}(2))=3</annotation></semantics></math> and we conclude <math id="S6.p22.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\leq 6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\leq 6</annotation></semantics></math>. By Hopf, we
must have <math id="S6.p22.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=6</annotation></semantics></math>. As in the case <math id="S6.p22.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e=2</annotation></semantics></math>, we can now use Eisenbud’s
result <a href="#S6.Thmthm5" title="Proposition 6.5. ‣ 6. Rational space curves admitting surfaces of decomposable lines ‣ Monodromy of Projective Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.5</span></a>(2) to conclude <math id="S6.p22.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=3</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p22.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is a rational
normal sextic curve.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmthm7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 6.7</span></span><span id="S6.Thmthm7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmthm7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The hypothesis <math id="S6.Thmthm7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\mathbb{P}^{1}\rightarrow\mathbb{P}(V^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\mathbb{P}^{1}\rightarrow\mathbb{P}(V^{*})</annotation></semantics></math> is an embedding - rather than
birational onto its image - is used only to
apply the theorem of Kaji (a general tangent to <math id="S6.Thmthm7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> intersects <math id="S6.Thmthm7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> only at the
point of tangency). But one can avoid using this fact analyzing what happens
if the projection from a general tangent line has degree less than <math id="S6.Thmthm7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2d-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2d-2</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmthm8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 6.8</span></span><span id="S6.Thmthm8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmthm8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In fact, in both the cases <math id="S6.Thmthm8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.Thmthm8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=3</annotation></semantics></math>, one sees <math id="S6.Thmthm8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(Y)=X\cap\mathbb{P}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(Y)=X\cap\mathbb{P}(V)</annotation></semantics></math>
for example using the fact <math id="S6.Thmthm8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{-1}(X\cap\mathbb{P}(V))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{-1}(X\cap\mathbb{P}(V))</annotation></semantics></math> is cut out by the right
number of quadrics. Thus <math id="S6.Thmthm8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> is unique, and the surface <math id="S6.Thmthm8.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> of decomposable
lines for <math id="S6.Thmthm8.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is determined as the dual of the families of lines in <math id="S6.Thmthm8.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(V)</annotation></semantics></math>
that are secant to the rational curve <math id="S6.Thmthm8.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(Y)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmthm9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 6.9</span></span><span id="S6.Thmthm9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmthm9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The proposition is false in positive characteristic.
An example in char. 7 of a normal rational quartic <math id="S6.Thmthm9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\!\subset\!\mathbb{P}_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">⊂</mo><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\!\subset\!\mathbb{P}_{4}</annotation></semantics></math>
such that <math id="S6.Thmthm9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(Y)</annotation></semantics></math> spans only a <math id="S6.Thmthm9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S6.Thmthm9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}_{8}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>8</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}_{8}</annotation></semantics></math> is given by the parametric equation</p>
<table id="S6.Ex44" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex44.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(t)=1-2tX-2t^{2}X^{2}-4t^{3}X^{3}+4t^{4}X^{4}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(t)=1-2tX-2t^{2}X^{2}-4t^{3}X^{3}+4t^{4}X^{4}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.Thmthm9.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Indeed <math id="S6.Thmthm9.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(Y)</annotation></semantics></math> is parametrized by</p>
<table id="S6.Ex45" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex45.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(t)^{2}=1-4tX+28t^{4}X^{4}-32t^{7}X^{7}+16t^{8}X^{8}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>28</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>32</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>7</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mn>7</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>8</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mn>8</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(t)^{2}=1-4tX+28t^{4}X^{4}-32t^{7}X^{7}+16t^{8}X^{8}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmthm10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 6.10</span></span><span id="S6.Thmthm10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmthm10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm10.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmthm10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose <math id="S6.Thmthm10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is a smooth rational curve having special monodromy. Then
<math id="S6.Thmthm10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg C</annotation></semantics></math> is either <math id="S6.Thmthm10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S6.Thmthm10.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore, every rational quartic
has special monodromy; the general rational sextic does not have special monodromy,
but there are rational sextics with special monodromy.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.6" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S6.p23" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p23.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p23.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S6.p23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> has special monodromy, its degree is an even number <math id="S6.p23.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2d\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2d\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math> and by Proposition <a href="#S6.Thmthm1" title="Proposition 6.1. ‣ 6. Rational space curves admitting surfaces of decomposable lines ‣ Monodromy of Projective Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></a> there is an irreducible curve
<math id="S6.p23.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\!\subset\!\mathbb{P}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">⊂</mo><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\!\subset\!\mathbb{P}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S6.p23.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(Y)\!\subset\!\mathbb{P}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">⊂</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(Y)\!\subset\!\mathbb{P}(V)</annotation></semantics></math>. By
Corollary <a href="#S6.Thmthm4" title="Corollary 6.4. ‣ 6. Rational space curves admitting surfaces of decomposable lines ‣ Monodromy of Projective Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.4</span></a> the curve <math id="S6.p23.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(Y)</annotation></semantics></math> spans <math id="S6.p23.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(V)</annotation></semantics></math>. Now
Proposition <a href="#S6.Thmthm6" title="Proposition 6.6. ‣ 6. Rational space curves admitting surfaces of decomposable lines ‣ Monodromy of Projective Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.6</span></a> implies <math id="S6.p23.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg(C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg(C)</annotation></semantics></math> is either <math id="S6.p23.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S6.p23.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p24" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p24.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p24.1.1" class="ltx_text">To see that every rational quartic has special monodromy, we observe the converse
of Proposition <a href="#S6.Thmthm1" title="Proposition 6.1. ‣ 6. Rational space curves admitting surfaces of decomposable lines ‣ Monodromy of Projective Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></a> holds: if <math id="S6.p24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\!\subset\!\mathbb{P}(V^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">⊂</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\!\subset\!\mathbb{P}(V^{*})</annotation></semantics></math> is a smooth rational curve of
degree <math id="S6.p24.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2d\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2d\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p24.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim X\cap\mathbb{P}(V)\,&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim X\cap\mathbb{P}(V)\,&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S6.p24.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> has special monodromy.
Indeed, suppose there is an irreducible curve <math id="S6.p24.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\subset X\cap\mathbb{P}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\subset X\cap\mathbb{P}(V)</annotation></semantics></math>.
By Corollary <a href="#S6.Thmthm4" title="Corollary 6.4. ‣ 6. Rational space curves admitting surfaces of decomposable lines ‣ Monodromy of Projective Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.4</span></a>, the curve <math id="S6.p24.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> spans <math id="S6.p24.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(V)</annotation></semantics></math>, that is, it is not contained
in a plane. Therefore the secant lines to <math id="S6.p24.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> are not all
contained in one plane. Now let <math id="S6.p24.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{\prime}\!\subset\!\mathbb{G}(1,\mathbb{P}(V))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">⊂</mo><mrow><mi>𝔾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{\prime}\!\subset\!\mathbb{G}(1,\mathbb{P}(V))</annotation></semantics></math> be the surface of secant lines to <math id="S6.p24.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, and let
<math id="S6.p24.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> be the corresponding subvariety of <math id="S6.p24.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{G}(1,\mathbb{P}(V^{*}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{G}(1,\mathbb{P}(V^{*}))</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since the secant lines to <math id="S6.p24.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> are not
all contained in one plane, <math id="S6.p24.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is not contained in a
Schubert cycle <math id="S6.p24.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(x)</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S6.p24.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(V)</annotation></semantics></math> is spanned by <math id="S6.p24.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, a general
secant line <math id="S6.p24.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S6.p24.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is base point free, so that its dual line
<math id="S6.p24.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\in\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\in\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> does not meet <math id="S6.p24.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore, by construction a general
line in <math id="S6.p24.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is decomposable as prescribed by special
monodromy. Therefore <math id="S6.p24.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> has special monodromy.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p25" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p25.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p25.1.1" class="ltx_text">Now suppose <math id="S6.p25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is a rational quartic, that is, <math id="S6.p25.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=2</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then <math id="S6.p25.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is a Veronese surface in <math id="S6.p25.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}_{4}</annotation></semantics></math>, and every
hyperplane <math id="S6.p25.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(V)</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S6.p25.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> cuts a curve on <math id="S6.p25.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus <math id="S6.p25.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> has special monodromy.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p26" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p26.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p26.1.1" class="ltx_text">When <math id="S6.p26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=3</annotation></semantics></math>, the quadratic Veronese <math id="S6.p26.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> has codimension <math id="S6.p26.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S6.p26.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}_{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}_{6}</annotation></semantics></math>, therefore a
general <math id="S6.p26.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S6.p26.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}_{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}_{6}</annotation></semantics></math> does not cut a curve on <math id="S6.p26.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, and the general
rational sextic does not have special monodromy. For an example of a sextic
with special monodromy, consider - cf. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> -
the twisted cubic curve <math id="S6.p26.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\!\subset\!\mathbb{P}^{3}\cong\mathbb{C}[X]_{\leq 3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\!\subset\!\mathbb{P}^{3}\cong\mathbb{C}[X]_{\leq 3}</annotation></semantics></math>,
image of the embedding <math id="S6.p26.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g:\mathbb{P}^{1}\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g:\mathbb{P}^{1}\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> defined by</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex46" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex46.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(t)=X+tX^{3}+t^{2}-t^{3}X^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(t)=X+tX^{3}+t^{2}-t^{3}X^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p26.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p26.2.1" class="ltx_text">One immediately checks <math id="S6.p26.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(Y)</annotation></semantics></math> spans a three dimensional subspace <math id="S6.p26.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(V)</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S6.p26.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}_{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}_{6}</annotation></semantics></math>,
and the linear series <math id="S6.p26.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|V|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|V|</annotation></semantics></math> defines an embedding of <math id="S6.p26.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S6.p26.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(V^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(V^{*})</annotation></semantics></math>: the image
<math id="S6.p26.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> of this embedding is a rational sextic with special monodromy. This example
can be constructed noting <math id="S6.p26.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(Y)</annotation></semantics></math>, as every sextic in <math id="S6.p26.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math>, must have quadrisecant lines,
and these must be degenerate because of Lemma <a href="#S6.Thmthm3" title="Lemma 6.3. ‣ 6. Rational space curves admitting surfaces of decomposable lines ‣ Monodromy of Projective Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.3</span></a>. Thus one looks
for <math id="S6.p26.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> such that the line joining <math id="S6.p26.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(0)^{2}=X^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(0)^{2}=X^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p26.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(\infty)^{2}=X^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(\infty)^{2}=X^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> is tangent to <math id="S6.p26.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(Y)</annotation></semantics></math>
both at <math id="S6.p26.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(0)^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(0)^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and at <math id="S6.p26.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(\infty)^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(\infty)^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7. </span>Examples of curves admitting a one dimensional family of non uniform lines</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We give examples of space curves having a one dimensional
family of non uniform lines.
The monodromy of all these lines
is contained in the alternating group.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We recall
the construction given in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>. Let <math id="S7.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be any smooth curve of genus <math id="S7.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S7.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> be a spin line bundle on <math id="S7.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X,</annotation></semantics></math> i.e.
<math id="S7.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{2}=\omega_{X}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{2}=\omega_{X}.</annotation></semantics></math> We fix a point <math id="S7.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S7.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> - in fact one could
take any effective divisor <math id="S7.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S7.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{m}=H^{0}(X,L(mP))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{m}=H^{0}(X,L(mP))</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S7.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{m}=H^{0}(X,\omega_{X}(2mP))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{m}=H^{0}(X,\omega_{X}(2mP))</annotation></semantics></math>.
Given a two plane <math id="S7.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi\subset V_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi\subset V_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, we let
<math id="S7.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{2}(\Pi)\subset W_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{2}(\Pi)\subset W_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> be the <math id="S7.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>−</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3-</annotation></semantics></math>dimensional space image of the natural map
<math id="S7.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi\otimes\Pi\to W_{m}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi\otimes\Pi\to W_{m}.</annotation></semantics></math> If <math id="S7.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(s,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(s,t)</annotation></semantics></math> is a base of <math id="S7.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi</annotation></semantics></math>,
we define a basis of <math id="S7.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{2}(\Pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{2}(\Pi)</annotation></semantics></math> setting <math id="S7.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{1}=s^{2}-t^{2}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{1}=s^{2}-t^{2},</annotation></semantics></math>
<math id="S7.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{2}=i(s^{2}+t^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{2}=i(s^{2}+t^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S7.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{3}=2st." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{3}=2st.</annotation></semantics></math> In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>, a plane <math id="S7.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi</annotation></semantics></math> is called <span id="S7.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">minimal</span> if all the elements
of <math id="S7.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{2}(\Pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{2}(\Pi)</annotation></semantics></math> are exact meromorphic forms.
Then there exist <math id="S7.p2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{i}\in H^{0}(X,\mathcal{O}_{X}(2m-1)P))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{i}\in H^{0}(X,\mathcal{O}_{X}(2m-1)P))</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="S7.p2.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="dF_{i}=\omega_{i}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dF_{i}=\omega_{i}.</annotation></semantics></math> The name minimal and the above choice of the basis
come from the fact the real parts of the <math id="S7.p2.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> define a map <math id="S7.p2.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G:X-P\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G:X-P\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
<table id="S7.Ex47" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex47.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(x)=Re(F_{1}(x),F_{2}(x),F_{3}(x))," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(x)=Re(F_{1}(x),F_{2}(x),F_{3}(x)),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.p2.2" class="ltx_p">which gives a minimal surface in the Euclidean space.
Associated to a minimal plane <math id="S7.p2.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi</annotation></semantics></math>
we consider the space:</p>
<table id="S7.Ex48" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex48.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W=\{k\in H^{0}(X,\mathcal{O}_{X}(2m-1)P):dk\in S^{2}(\Pi)\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W=\{k\in H^{0}(X,\mathcal{O}_{X}(2m-1)P):dk\in S^{2}(\Pi)\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.p2.3" class="ltx_p">Note that <math id="S7.p2.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> is generated
by the constants and the <math id="S7.p2.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. If moreover the linear system associated
to <math id="S7.p2.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi</annotation></semantics></math> is base point free, then
<math id="S7.p2.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> is also base point free and it defines an immersion
<math id="S7.p2.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:X\to\mathbb{P}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:X\to\mathbb{P}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> setting <math id="S7.p2.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x)=(1,F_{1},F_{2},F_{3})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x)=(1,F_{1},F_{2},F_{3}).</annotation></semantics></math>
For suitable choices (for instance if 2m-1 is a prime number)
<math id="S7.p2.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(X)</annotation></semantics></math> is a curve of degree 2m-1 and hence <math id="S7.p2.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is birational onto
its image.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The existence, for any <math id="S7.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>,
of base point free minimal planes <math id="S7.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi\subset V_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi\subset V_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> is proven
in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, Proposition 5.8 p. 355]</cite>
for <math id="S7.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m&gt;31g+21" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>31</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>21</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m&gt;31g+21</annotation></semantics></math>.
On the other hand, if <math id="S7.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\in\Pi,\sigma\neq 0," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\in\Pi,\sigma\neq 0,</annotation></semantics></math> let
<math id="S7.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\sigma}\subset W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\sigma}\subset W</annotation></semantics></math> be the two
dimensional subspace defined by the equation
<math id="S7.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="dF=\lambda\sigma^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dF=\lambda\sigma^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\in\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\in\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>. Note
<math id="S7.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\sigma}=\langle 1,F_{\sigma}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\sigma}=\langle 1,F_{\sigma}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S7.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="dF_{\sigma}=\sigma^{2}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dF_{\sigma}=\sigma^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math>
Now <math id="S7.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> defines a non uniform line
for <math id="S7.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(X)</annotation></semantics></math>. In fact the monodromy of
<math id="S7.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> is contained in the alternating group. This follows because all
the ramifications
<math id="S7.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> are odd. The map <math id="S7.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\to L_{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\to L_{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math>
defines a <math id="S7.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}=\mathbb{P}(\Pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}=\mathbb{P}(\Pi)</annotation></semantics></math> family of non uniform lines
for <math id="S7.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=f(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=f(X)</annotation></semantics></math>.
We remark that all the lines belong to a fixed plane.</p>
</div>
</section>
